(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["trees",{"l:trees/what-do-pre-order-in-order-and-post-order-visit-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A depth-first traversal visits the whole tree with one recursive function; pre-order, in-order and post-order differ only in \u003cb>when\u003c/b> the node itself is handled: before its children, between them, or after them. Choose the order by what the node needs: facts from above (pre-order), its children's results (post-order), or sorted order in a search tree (in-order). All three cost O(n) time and O(h) stack, where h is the height.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Picture walking around the outside of the tree, starting at the root and keeping the tree on your left, like tracing its outline with a pencil. You pass every node three times: once on the way down its left side, once underneath it (between its two subtrees), and once on the way back up its right side.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>          1            Trace the outline anticlockwise from the root:\n        /   \\            pre-order  lists a node the FIRST time you pass it\n       2     3           in-order   lists it when you pass UNDER it\n      / \\     \\          post-order lists it the LAST time you pass it\n     4   5     6\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>That single walk is the recursion. The three orders are just three places to write \u003ccode>out.append(node.val)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Vocabulary you will use all module\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>A \u003cb>node\u003c/b> holds a value and links to at most two \u003cb>children\u003c/b>, called left and right (the order matters). The node above is its \u003cb>parent\u003c/b>. The top node is the \u003cb>root\u003c/b>; a node with no children is a \u003cb>leaf\u003c/b>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The \u003cb>subtree\u003c/b> of a node is that node plus all its descendants. Every recursive tree function is a statement about subtrees.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The \u003cb>depth\u003c/b> of a node is the number of edges from the root to it (the root has depth 0).\u003c/li>\u003cli>The \u003cb>height\u003c/b> of a tree, in this module, is the number of nodes on its longest root-to-leaf path, so an empty tree has height 0 and a single node height 1. This matches LeetCode's \"maximum depth\"; some books count edges instead, which is one less. Diameters (later card) count edges.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A tree with height h holds at most 2^h - 1 nodes, so n nodes need height at least log2(n + 1). A tree is \u003cb>balanced\u003c/b> when its height stays O(log n); a \u003cb>degenerate\u003c/b> tree is a chain of height n.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>Three ways to store a tree\u003c/h5>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Linked nodes\u003c/b>: a \u003ccode>TreeNode\u003c/code> object with \u003ccode>val\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>right\u003c/code>. This is what interview problems hand you, and what you use when the shape is irregular.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>An array, for complete trees\u003c/b>: store the levels left to right; the children of index i are 2i + 1 and 2i + 2, and its parent is (i - 1) // 2. No pointers at all. Binary heaps use exactly this layout.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A parent array or child lists, for general trees\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>parent[v]\u003c/code> for each vertex, or \u003ccode>children[v]\u003c/code> as a list. File systems, org charts and \"n nodes and n - 1 edges\" inputs arrive like this.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>LeetCode prints trees as a level-order list with \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> for missing children, such as \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code> for the tree above. The next card gives a builder for that format.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>The walk\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The function below makes one depth-first pass and records each node at all three moments. Keep only the line you need and you have the traversal you need.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count the calls. \u003ccode>walk\u003c/code> runs once for each of the n nodes and once for each empty child slot. A tree with n nodes has 2n child slots, of which n - 1 hold a node (every node except the root is someone's child), so n + 1 slots are empty. That is 2n + 1 calls, each doing O(1) work apart from its own two calls, so the traversal is \u003cb>O(n) time\u003c/b>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Space is the call stack. At any moment the stack holds the frames on one root-to-node path, so its size is at most h + 1: \u003cb>O(h) space\u003c/b>, about log2 n for a balanced tree and n for a chain. In Python the default recursion limit is 1000 frames, so a chain-shaped tree of a few thousand nodes crashes the recursive version; the card on iterative traversal fixes that.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Order\u003c/th>\u003cth>Node handled\u003c/th>\u003cth>Use it when\u003c/th>\u003cth>Examples\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Pre-order\u003c/td>\u003ctd>before its children\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the node must act before its subtree: copy, print an outline, pass facts down\u003c/td>\u003ctd>serialising a tree, printing a folder outline\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>In-order\u003c/td>\u003ctd>between left and right\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the left-node-right order means something\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sorted keys of a binary search tree, an infix expression\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Post-order\u003c/td>\u003ctd>after its children\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the node needs its children's results\u003c/td>\u003ctd>heights, sizes, deleting a tree, evaluating an expression\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Level order\u003c/td>\u003ctd>by depth\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the question is about levels or the nearest node\u003c/td>\u003ctd>next card\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Do not use a depth-first traversal when the question is about levels or the shallowest match (breadth-first finds the nearest leaf without exploring deep branches), or when the tree may be very deep and you are in Python (use an explicit stack).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One walk over the tree above, recording all three orders. Watch the call stack: it is exactly the path from the root to the current node.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Call walk(1). First touch of 1, so pre-order records it; then walk descends to the left child\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  walk(1)\npre    \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\nin     -\npost   -\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">walk(2): its first touch puts 2 into pre-order, and the walk descends left again\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  walk(1) &gt; walk(2)\npre    1 \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\nin     -\npost   -\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">walk(4) is a leaf: both child calls return at once, so its three touches happen back to back\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  walk(1) &gt; walk(2) &gt; walk(4)\npre    1 2 \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\nin     \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\npost   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Back in walk(2), the left call has returned, so 2 goes into in-order; then walk(5), another leaf\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  walk(1) &gt; walk(2) &gt; walk(5)\npre    1 2 4 \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>\nin     4 \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark> \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>\npost   4 \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">walk(2) finishes: its last touch puts 2 into post-order. Back in walk(1), the left side is done, so 1 goes into in-order\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  walk(1)\npre    1 2 4 5\nin     4 2 5 \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\npost   4 5 \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">walk(3): pre-order 3; its left child is None, so its in-order touch comes immediately; then the leaf 6\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  walk(1) &gt; walk(3) &gt; walk(6)\npre    1 2 4 5 \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark> \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>\nin     4 2 5 1 \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark> \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>\npost   4 5 2 \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">walk(3) and then walk(1) finish, appending 3 and 1 to post-order. Every node was touched three times\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  (empty)\npre    1 2 4 5 3 6\nin     4 2 5 1 3 6\npost   4 5 2 6 \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark> \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class TreeNode:\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.left = left\n        self.right = right\n\n\ndef traverse(root):\n    \"\"\"Return (preorder, inorder, postorder) value lists from one depth-first walk.\"\"\"\n    pre, ino, post = [], [], []\n\n    def walk(node):\n        if node is None:\n            return\n        pre.append(node.val)      # first touch: before the children\n        walk(node.left)\n        ino.append(node.val)      # second touch: between the children\n        walk(node.right)\n        post.append(node.val)     # last touch: after the children\n\n    walk(root)\n    return pre, ino, post\n\n\nroot = TreeNode(1, TreeNode(2, TreeNode(4), TreeNode(5)), TreeNode(3, None, TreeNode(6)))\nprint(traverse(root))\n# ([1, 2, 4, 5, 3, 6], [4, 2, 5, 1, 3, 6], [4, 5, 2, 6, 3, 1])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Appending to lists owned by the outer function keeps the whole walk O(n). For a single order, delete the other two appends.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Concatenating lists\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>return [node.val] + pre(node.left) + pre(node.right)\u003c/code> copies every sublist at every level. That is O(n log n) on a balanced tree and O(n^2) on a chain. Append to one shared list instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No base case\u003c/b>: forgetting \u003ccode>if node is None: return\u003c/code> gives \u003ccode>AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'left'\u003c/code> at the first leaf.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Depth versus height\u003c/b>: depth is measured down from the root, height up from the leaves, and books disagree on counting nodes or edges. Write your convention in the docstring.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>In-order needs two children\u003c/b>: it is defined for binary trees only. For a node with many children, only pre-order and post-order make sense.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mutable defaults\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>def walk(node, out=[])\u003c/code> shares one list across every call of the function, including later test cases.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Recursion depth\u003c/b>: Python raises \u003ccode>RecursionError\u003c/code> near 1000 nested calls. LeetCode-sized trees (up to 10^4 nodes) can be that deep if they are skewed.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A binary tree has pre-order \u003ccode>A B D E C F\u003c/code> and in-order \u003ccode>D B E A C F\u003c/code>. What is its post-order?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>D E B F C A\u003c/code>. Pre-order says A is the root. In in-order, D B E lies left of A and C F right of it. The left part's pre-order is B D E, so B is its root with D on the left and E on the right. The right part's pre-order is C F and in-order C F, so C has no left child and F is its right child. Post-order is left subtree, right subtree, root: D E B, F C, A.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">\u003ccode>walk\u003c/code> is called 2n + 1 times. Why is the traversal still O(n), and why is the extra space O(h) rather than O(n)?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each call does a constant amount of work besides making its two child calls, and 2n + 1 is O(n). A frame is popped as soon as its subtree is finished, so the only frames alive at any moment are those on the path from the root to the current node: at most h + 1 of them.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which order would you use to (a) print a folder outline with each folder above its contents, (b) compute the total size of every folder, (c) list a binary search tree's keys in increasing order?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(a) Pre-order: a folder is printed before anything inside it. (b) Post-order: a folder's total needs its children's totals first. (c) In-order: in a binary search tree everything left of a node is smaller and everything right is larger, so left, node, right comes out sorted.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A colleague's \u003ccode>preorder(node)\u003c/code> returns \u003ccode>[node.val] + preorder(node.left) + preorder(node.right)\u003c/code>. It is fine on the tests but times out on a 30,000-node input. Why, and what is the fix?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The large input is probably a chain. Each level copies the whole list returned from below, so the total copying is about 1 + 2 + ... + n, which is O(n^2), around 450 million element copies at n = 30,000. (It also exceeds the recursion limit unless that was raised.) Append to a single shared list, as in the template, for O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>94\u003c/b>: return the in-order values recursively by keeping only the middle \u003ccode>append\u003c/code> of the template. Expected: for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code> the answer is \u003ccode>[4, 2, 5, 1, 3, 6]\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code> for an empty tree; O(n) time and O(h) extra space. You will solve it again without recursion later in this module.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>104\u003c/b>: compute the maximum depth with a post-order function (each node needs its children's depths first). Expected: 3 for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code>, 0 for an empty tree, and 4 for the chain \u003ccode>[1, 2, None, 3, None, 4]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to store a binary tree, walk it depth-first in O(n), and choose pre-, in- or post-order by when a node needs its information.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:trees/how-do-you-walk-a-tree-level-by-level-and-why-do":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Breadth-first search (BFS) visits nodes in order of depth: the root, then all its children, then all grandchildren. A FIFO queue produces this order automatically, because children join the back of the queue behind every node already waiting. Taking \u003ccode>len(queue)\u003c/code> before each round splits the stream into levels. It costs O(n) time and O(w) memory, where w is the widest level.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Drop a stone in a pond: the ripple reaches everything at distance 1, then distance 2, and never jumps ahead. Or think of an org chart read rank by rank: the CEO, then every direct report, then every report of those. A queue is the waiting line that enforces this: whoever was discovered earlier is served earlier, and a node's children are discovered only when the node is served, so they always wait behind the rest of the current level.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A stack would serve the newest node first and dive deep, which is depth-first order. Swapping the container is the only difference between the two traversals.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Put the root in a \u003ccode>collections.deque\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>While the queue is not empty, read \u003ccode>size = len(queue)\u003c/code>. Exactly that many nodes form the current level, because every one of them was enqueued while the previous level was processed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Pop \u003ccode>size\u003c/code> nodes from the front, record them, and push their children on the back. The children belong to the next level and are not touched in this round.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Store the finished level and repeat.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Many questions are \"BFS plus one line per level\": the \u003cb>right side view\u003c/b> is the last value of each level, the average or maximum of each row is a reduction over the level list, and the \u003cb>minimum depth\u003c/b> is the level number of the first leaf you pop, at which point you can stop.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For width questions where gaps count, give each node a position as in an array-backed heap (children of i at 2i and 2i + 1) and measure last minus first plus one per level. Subtract each level's first position from the positions on that level so the numbers stay small.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Level order without a queue\u003c/h5>\u003cp>You can also group by level with depth-first recursion: pass \u003ccode>depth\u003c/code> down and append the value to \u003ccode>levels[depth]\u003c/code>, creating the list the first time a depth is reached. Same output, O(h) stack instead of O(w) queue. Use whichever is lighter for the tree's shape.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>The LeetCode list format\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code> is a level-order listing in which \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> marks a missing child, and the children of missing nodes are simply not listed. It is \u003cb>not\u003c/b> heap indexing: in \u003ccode>[1, None, 2, 3]\u003c/code>, the 3 is the left child of 2, even though index 3 would be a child of index 1 in an array heap. The builder below consumes the list with the same queue discipline that produced it, so you can make test trees in one line.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node is appended to the queue once and popped once, both O(1) on a deque, and each pop does O(1) work: \u003cb>O(n) time\u003c/b>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The queue holds at most one level plus part of the next, so memory is \u003cb>O(w)\u003c/b> for the widest level w. On a perfect tree the last level holds (n + 1) / 2 nodes, so w is about n/2 and BFS needs O(n) memory, while depth-first search needs only O(log n). On a chain the opposite holds: w = 1 but h = n.\u003c/p>\u003cp>With a plain list and \u003ccode>pop(0)\u003c/code>, every pop shifts the remaining elements, so the traversal becomes O(n^2) on wide trees.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Question\u003c/th>\u003cth>Prefer\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Anything \"per level\" or \"per row\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS\u003c/td>\u003ctd>levels fall out of the level-size loop\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Nearest node, minimum depth\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS\u003c/td>\u003ctd>stops at the first match without exploring deep branches\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>An answer built from subtree answers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DFS\u003c/td>\u003ctd>children's results are ready in post-order\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Memory on a huge balanced tree\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DFS\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(log n) stack against O(n) queue\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Memory on a deep, skinny tree\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1)-ish queue against O(n) stack\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The same loop, with a visited set, is breadth-first search on graphs, where it finds shortest paths in unweighted graphs. You will reuse it unchanged in the graphs module.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The tree from the previous card, \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start: the queue holds only the root, so len(queue) = 1 and level 0 has one node\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue   [\u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>]\nlevel   []\nlevels  []\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop 1, record it, push its children 2 and 3. One pop done: level 0 is complete\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue   [\u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>]\nlevel   [1]\nlevels  [\u003cmark>[1]\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">len(queue) = 2, so level 1 has two nodes. Pop 2 and push 4 and 5 behind 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue   [3, \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>]\nlevel   [\u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>]\nlevels  [[1]]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop 3 and push its only child 6. Two pops done: level 1 is complete, and the queue now holds exactly level 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue   [4, 5, \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>]\nlevel   [2, \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>]\nlevels  [[1], \u003cmark>[2, 3]\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">len(queue) = 3: pop 4, 5 and 6. None has children, so nothing is pushed\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue   []\nlevel   [\u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>]\nlevels  [[1], [2, 3], \u003cmark>[4, 5, 6]\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The queue is empty, so the walk ends. Its peak size was 3, the width of the widest level\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue   []\nlevels  [[1], [2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\n\ndef level_order(root):\n    \"\"\"Values grouped by depth: [[root], [depth 1, ...], ...].\"\"\"\n    if root is None:\n        return []\n    levels, queue = [], deque([root])\n    while queue:\n        level = []\n        for _ in range(len(queue)):        # exactly the nodes of this level\n            node = queue.popleft()\n            level.append(node.val)\n            if node.left:\n                queue.append(node.left)\n            if node.right:\n                queue.append(node.right)\n        levels.append(level)\n    return levels\n\n\ndef build(values):\n    \"\"\"Tree from a LeetCode-style level-order list such as [1, 2, 3, None, 5].\"\"\"\n    if not values or values[0] is None:\n        return None\n    root = TreeNode(values[0])\n    queue, i = deque([root]), 1\n    while queue and i &lt; len(values):\n        node = queue.popleft()\n        if values[i] is not None:\n            node.left = TreeNode(values[i])\n            queue.append(node.left)\n        i += 1\n        if i &lt; len(values) and values[i] is not None:\n            node.right = TreeNode(values[i])\n            queue.append(node.right)\n        i += 1\n    return root\n\n\ndef to_list(root):\n    \"\"\"Inverse of build: level order with None gaps, trailing Nones removed.\"\"\"\n    out, queue = [], deque([root])\n    while queue:\n        node = queue.popleft()\n        if node is None:\n            out.append(None)\n            continue\n        out.append(node.val)\n        queue.append(node.left)\n        queue.append(node.right)\n    while out and out[-1] is None:\n        out.pop()\n    return out\n\n\nprint(level_order(build([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6])))   # [[1], [2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\nprint(to_list(build([1, None, 2, 3])))                # [1, None, 2, 3]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>build\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>to_list\u003c/code> use the \u003ccode>TreeNode\u003c/code> class from the previous card. \u003ccode>range(len(queue))\u003c/code> is evaluated once, before the loop starts, so children pushed during the round do not extend it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>list.pop(0)\u003c/code>\u003c/b> is O(n). Use \u003ccode>deque.popleft()\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Merging levels\u003c/b>: replacing the \u003ccode>for _ in range(len(queue))\u003c/code> loop with \u003ccode>while queue\u003c/code> processes everything as one level. You still get level order, but lose the grouping.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pushing \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> children\u003c/b> forces a check on every pop. Either skip them when pushing (as \u003ccode>level_order\u003c/code> does) or skip them when popping (as \u003ccode>to_list\u003c/code> does, because it needs the gaps), but do it consistently.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Heap indexing on LeetCode lists\u003c/b>: children of \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> are omitted, so index arithmetic like 2i + 1 does not find children in that format.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Empty tree\u003c/b>: return \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code> before creating the queue, or you will pop \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> and crash on \u003ccode>.val\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exploding positions in width problems\u003c/b>: positions double every level, so subtract the level's first position at each level to keep them small (Python will not overflow, but the numbers grow huge on deep trees, and C++ or Java do overflow).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">For the tree \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, None, 4, 5, 6, None, None, 7]\u003c/code>, what are the queue contents just after level 1 has been fully processed, and what does \u003ccode>level_order\u003c/code> return?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Level 1 is nodes 2 and 3. Popping 2 pushes its only child 4 (its left is None); popping 3 pushes 5 and 6. The queue is then \u003ccode>[4, 5, 6]\u003c/code>. Node 4 has no children here (the two Nones), 5 has left child 7, so the result is \u003ccode>[[1], [2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7]]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does BFS find the minimum depth of a tree faster than DFS on a tree with one shallow leaf and one very deep branch?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>BFS pops nodes in order of depth, so the first leaf it pops is a shallowest leaf and it can return at once, after visiting only the levels above it. DFS has no such guarantee: it may descend the deep branch first, and it must keep exploring (at best pruning branches deeper than the best leaf found so far) before it knows which leaf is shallowest.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You need, for every level, the largest value. Your tree is perfect with 2^20 - 1 nodes. BFS or DFS with a depth parameter, and why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Both are O(n) time, but BFS keeps the whole last level in its queue: 2^19, over half a million node references. DFS with \u003ccode>best[depth] = max(...)\u003c/code> keeps only about 20 frames plus a 20-entry list. DFS is lighter here; BFS would win on a deep, skinny tree.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>build([1, None, 2, 3])\u003c/code> produce, and why is the 3 not a child of the root?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The root 1 has no left child and a right child 2, and 2 has left child 3. After the root, values are consumed two at a time for each node popped from the queue: \u003ccode>None, 2\u003c/code> are the root's children, and \u003ccode>3\u003c/code> is the left child of the next node popped, which is 2. The missing left child of the root uses up its own slot (the \u003ccode>None\u003c/code>) but never enters the queue, so it has no slots for children.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>102\u003c/b>: return the values grouped by level with the template. Expected: \u003ccode>[[1], [2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code> for an empty tree, and \u003ccode>[[1], [2], [3]]\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>[1, None, 2, 3]\u003c/code>; O(n) time and O(w) extra space.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>104\u003c/b>: solve maximum depth again, this time by counting how many levels BFS processes. Expected: 3 for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code> and 3 for \u003ccode>[1, None, 2, 3]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to traverse a tree level by level with a deque, split it into levels with the level-size loop, and build test trees from level-order lists.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:trees/what-should-each-recursive-call-return-solving-t":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Most tree problems are solved by one function \u003ccode>f(node)\u003c/code> that \u003cb>returns a fact about the subtree rooted at node\u003c/b>. Write that fact down as a contract, choose what an empty tree returns, and compute a node's answer from its children's answers, trusting the recursive calls. If the parent needs more than the contract gives, \u003cb>return more\u003c/b> (a tuple or a sentinel) instead of calling a second walk: that keeps the whole solution O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Ask a manager for the head count of her department. She does not count everyone herself: she asks each team lead below her for their team's count, adds them, and adds one for herself. Each team lead does the same. The question flows down, the answers flow up, and nobody does more than a little addition. That is information \u003cb>flowing up\u003c/b>: an answer at a node depends only on answers about its subtrees.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The leap of faith is the same as in any recursion: assume the calls on the children already return correct answers for their subtrees (the induction hypothesis), and only worry about combining them. If the empty tree is handled correctly and the combine step is correct, induction on the height proves the whole function correct.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The \"what does each call return?\" template\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Answer four questions before writing any code:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Contract\u003c/b>: \"f(node) returns ___ about the subtree rooted at node.\" Write it as the docstring.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Empty tree\u003c/b>: what is f(None)? Pick the value that makes the combine step work with no special cases: 0 for heights, sizes and sums, True for \"every node satisfies...\", negative infinity for a maximum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Combine\u003c/b>: f(node) from f(node.left), f(node.right) and node.val. This is the only real thinking.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Report\u003c/b>: the answer is f(root) or one part of it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Question\u003c/th>\u003cth>f(node) returns\u003c/th>\u003cth>f(None)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Combine\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Number of nodes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>size of the subtree\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1 + L + R\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Maximum depth\u003c/td>\u003ctd>nodes on the longest downward path\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1 + max(L, R)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sum of values\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sum of the subtree\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>val + L + R\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Largest value\u003c/td>\u003ctd>maximum in the subtree\u003c/td>\u003ctd>-inf\u003c/td>\u003ctd>max(val, L, R)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Mirror the tree\u003c/td>\u003ctd>root of the mirrored subtree\u003c/td>\u003ctd>None\u003c/td>\u003ctd>swap the mirrored children\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Is it height-balanced?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>height, or -1 if unbalanced anywhere\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>-1 if L or R is -1 or abs(L - R) &gt; 1, else 1 + max(L, R)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>When the contract is too weak, strengthen it\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\"Is the tree height-balanced?\" (every node's two subtrees differ in height by at most one) cannot be answered from two booleans. Both subtrees can be balanced while one has height 1 and the other height 3. The parent needs the heights too. You have two choices:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Return a tuple\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>(balanced, height)\u003c/code>. Clear and general.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Encode with a sentinel\u003c/b>: return the height, or -1 meaning \"unbalanced somewhere below\". It works because -1 is never a real height, and it lets the function stop early.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>The slow alternative, calling a separate \u003ccode>height()\u003c/code> at every node, re-walks each subtree once per ancestor. That is O(n log n) on a balanced tree and O(n^2) on a chain. Strengthening the return value is the standard cure, and it is the seed of tree dynamic programming: in the advanced DP module, nodes return small tables instead of single numbers.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Two trees at once\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Comparing two trees is the same pattern with two arguments walked in lockstep: \u003ccode>same(a, b)\u003c/code> returns whether the subtrees rooted at a and b are identical. If either is None, they are equal only if both are; otherwise compare values and recurse on \u003ccode>(a.left, b.left)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>(a.right, b.right)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every call does O(1) work besides its two child calls, and there is one call per node plus one per empty slot, so these functions are \u003cb>O(n) time\u003c/b> and \u003cb>O(h) stack\u003c/b>. The sentinel version of the balance check can be faster still: once a subtree reports -1, its ancestors return at once without visiting the remaining subtrees.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For contrast, the naive balance check \u003ccode>abs(height(l) - height(r)) &lt;= 1 and balanced(l) and balanced(r)\u003c/code> calls \u003ccode>height\u003c/code> on every subtree from every ancestor. A node at depth d is measured d times, so the total is the sum of all depths: O(n log n) balanced, about n^2/2 on a chain.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use the bottom-up pattern whenever a node's answer depends on its \u003cb>descendants\u003c/b>: sizes, heights, sums, \"does this subtree contain X\", \"is this subtree a valid search tree\", removing or freeing nodes, and every tree DP. The cue in a problem statement is a quantity defined for \"the subtree rooted at each node\", or an answer that combines left and right results.\u003c/p>\u003cp>It is the wrong tool when a node's answer depends on its \u003cb>ancestors\u003c/b> (its depth, the maximum on the path from the root, a running sum). That information has to flow down as an argument, which is the next card. Many problems use both directions at once.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>h(node)\u003c/code> from the balance check, on this tree (\u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, None, None, None, 5]\u003c/code>):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>          1\n         / \\\n        2   3\n       /\n      4\n     /\n    5\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">h(1) calls h(2), which calls h(4), which calls h(5). Nothing can be decided until a leaf answers\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack    h(1) &gt; h(2) &gt; h(4) &gt; \u003cmark>h(5)\u003c/mark>\nreturned (none yet)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">h(5): both children are None (height 0), the difference is 0, so it returns 1 + max(0, 0) = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack    h(1) &gt; h(2) &gt; h(4)\nreturned 5 -&gt; \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">h(4): left 1, right 0. abs(1 - 0) &lt;= 1, so the subtree at 4 is balanced and has height 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack    h(1) &gt; h(2)\nreturned 5 -&gt; 1, 4 -&gt; \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">h(2): left 2, right 0. abs(2 - 0) = 2 &gt; 1: unbalanced, so it returns the sentinel -1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack    h(1)\nreturned 5 -&gt; 1, 4 -&gt; 2, 2 -&gt; \u003cmark>-1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">h(1): its left answer is -1, so it returns -1 at once. h(3) is never called, and is_balanced returns False\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack    (empty)\nreturned 5 -&gt; 1, 4 -&gt; 2, 2 -&gt; -1, 1 -&gt; \u003cmark>-1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def size(node):\n    \"\"\"Number of nodes in the subtree rooted at node.\"\"\"\n    if node is None:\n        return 0\n    return 1 + size(node.left) + size(node.right)\n\n\ndef height(node):\n    \"\"\"Nodes on the longest downward path from node (0 for an empty tree).\"\"\"\n    if node is None:\n        return 0\n    return 1 + max(height(node.left), height(node.right))\n\n\ndef is_balanced(root):\n    \"\"\"True if at every node the two subtree heights differ by at most one.\"\"\"\n    def h(node):\n        \"\"\"Height of the subtree, or -1 if it is unbalanced anywhere.\"\"\"\n        if node is None:\n            return 0\n        left = h(node.left)\n        if left &lt; 0:\n            return -1\n        right = h(node.right)\n        if right &lt; 0 or abs(left - right) &gt; 1:\n            return -1\n        return 1 + max(left, right)\n\n    return h(root) &gt;= 0\n\n\ndef same_tree(a, b):\n    \"\"\"True if the trees rooted at a and b have the same shape and values.\"\"\"\n    if a is None or b is None:\n        return a is b\n    return a.val == b.val and same_tree(a.left, b.left) and same_tree(a.right, b.right)\n\n\ndef invert(node):\n    \"\"\"Mirror the subtree in place and return its root.\"\"\"\n    if node is None:\n        return None\n    node.left, node.right = invert(node.right), invert(node.left)\n    return node\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In \u003ccode>invert\u003c/code>, the right-hand side is evaluated completely before either attribute is assigned, so both recursive calls see the original children.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>A walk inside the walk\u003c/b>: calling \u003ccode>height()\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>size()\u003c/code> from inside another recursive function multiplies the cost by the depth. Return the extra fact instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A base value that breaks the combine\u003c/b>: returning 0 for the maximum of an empty tree is wrong when values can be negative; use \u003ccode>float(\"-inf\")\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Sequential swap\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>node.left = invert(node.right)\u003c/code> followed by \u003ccode>node.right = invert(node.left)\u003c/code> reads the already-replaced left child, so both sides end up as the mirrored right subtree. Use one tuple assignment.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Short-circuit surprises\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>return f(node.left) and f(node.right)\u003c/code> skips the right call when the left is False. That is a useful early exit for pure functions and a bug if \u003ccode>f\u003c/code> also updates a counter.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Depth and height mixed up\u003c/b>: depth is a property of a node measured from above; height is a property of a subtree measured from below. Only height is a bottom-up quantity.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Run \u003ccode>height\u003c/code> on the tree \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code>. What does each call return?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The leaves 4, 5 and 6 return 1 (both children 0). Node 2 returns 1 + max(1, 1) = 2. Node 3 returns 1 + max(0, 1) = 2. The root returns 1 + max(2, 2) = 3.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is it correct for \u003ccode>is_balanced\u003c/code>'s helper to return -1 without even visiting the right subtree when the left reports -1?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The contract says -1 means \"unbalanced somewhere in this subtree\". If the left subtree is unbalanced, the node's subtree contains that unbalanced node too, so -1 is the correct answer for the node whatever the right subtree looks like. Skipping the right call only saves work.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Design the contract for \"the largest absolute difference between a node's value and the value of any of its descendants\". What does each call return, and where is the answer formed?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Let f(node) return the (minimum, maximum) of the subtree. At each node with children, the best difference involving that node as the ancestor is \u003ccode>max(abs(val - lo), abs(val - hi))\u003c/code> over the children's combined minimum and maximum; record the largest such value in an outer variable, and return \u003ccode>(min(val, lo), max(val, hi))\u003c/code> upward. An empty child contributes (inf, -inf). Everything is computed in one O(n) post-order pass.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">This invert passes on a single node but fails on \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3]\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>node.left = invert(node.right)\u003c/code> then \u003ccode>node.right = invert(node.left)\u003c/code>. What does it return for that tree, and why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It returns 1 with 3 on both sides, \u003ccode>[1, 3, 3]\u003c/code>. The first line sets \u003ccode>node.left\u003c/code> to the subtree 3. The second line then inverts \u003ccode>node.left\u003c/code>, which is now 3, not 2, so the right child also becomes 3 and node 2 is lost. Evaluate both calls before assigning: \u003ccode>node.left, node.right = invert(node.right), invert(node.left)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>104\u003c/b>: maximum depth with the \u003ccode>height\u003c/code> template. Expected: 3 for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code>, 4 for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, None, None, None, 5]\u003c/code>, and 0 for an empty tree; O(n) time, O(h) space.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>110\u003c/b>: height-balanced check with the -1 sentinel. Expected: True for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code>, False for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, None, None, None, 5]\u003c/code> (node 2 has subtree heights 2 and 0), True for an empty tree; O(n) time and O(h) space, with no second height walk.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to solve a tree problem bottom-up by fixing what each call returns, and how to strengthen that return value instead of re-walking subtrees.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:trees/when-should-information-flow-down-a-tree-instead":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>When a node's answer depends on its \u003cb>ancestors\u003c/b> (its depth, the largest value above it, the sum of the path from the root), pass that fact down as an \u003cb>argument\u003c/b>: each call receives what the path above knows and hands an updated copy to its children. Immutable arguments (numbers, tuples) undo themselves when the call returns; shared mutable state (a path list, a counter of prefix sums) must be undone explicitly on the way back up.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Walk down a mountain trail and keep a note of the highest point you have passed. At each viewpoint you can tell immediately whether it is the highest so far, using only your note. Your note is the argument. When the trail forks, you give each branch its own copy of the note; returning to the fork, you are back to the note you had there, because the branches never wrote on yours.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Compare this with the previous card. There, a node waited for its children to report (information up). Here, a node is told by its parent (information down). The two are often combined: the argument goes down, a count comes back up.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The template: state in, answer out\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>walk(node, state):\n    if node is None: return the neutral answer\n    look at node using state          (is it good? does a path end here?)\n    new_state = update(state, node)   (what the children will be told)\n    return combine(here, walk(left, new_state), walk(right, new_state))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Typical states and what they solve:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>State passed down\u003c/th>\u003cth>Update\u003c/th>\u003cth>Solves\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>depth\u003c/td>\u003ctd>depth + 1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>depth of every node, nodes at depth k\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>largest value on the path\u003c/td>\u003ctd>max(best, val)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"good\" nodes (no larger ancestor)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>sum of the root path\u003c/td>\u003ctd>running + val\u003c/td>\u003ctd>root-to-leaf path sum\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>number spelled by the path\u003c/td>\u003ctd>number * 10 + val\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sum of root-to-leaf numbers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>allowed range (low, high)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>narrow at each step\u003c/td>\u003ctd>validating a binary search tree (next module)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>the path itself (a list)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>append, recurse, pop\u003c/td>\u003ctd>listing all root-to-leaf paths\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>Two directions in one problem\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Counting good nodes uses both: the largest value so far flows down, and each call returns how many good nodes its subtree has. Most \"count the nodes such that...\" problems look like this.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Paths that start anywhere: prefix sums along the root path\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Counting downward paths with a given sum, starting at any node and ending at any node below it, looks hard: there are O(n h) such paths. Pass the running root-path sum down and keep a counter of the prefix sums on the \u003cb>current\u003c/b> root path. A downward path ending at the current node with sum \u003ccode>target\u003c/code> exists for every ancestor-prefix equal to \u003ccode>running - target\u003c/code>, exactly like counting subarrays with a given sum in an array (the prefix sums module). The tree twist: when the call returns, the current prefix must be removed from the counter, otherwise a sibling branch would pair with it and count a path that goes up and then down.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every pattern here is one pass: \u003cb>O(n) time\u003c/b>, \u003cb>O(h) stack\u003c/b>. The prefix counter adds O(1) expected work per node for its dictionary operations, so the path-sum count is O(n) instead of the O(n h) brute force that starts a walk at every node. The counter only ever holds counts for the current root path, though \u003ccode>Counter\u003c/code> keeps keys that dropped to zero; delete them if memory matters.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Watch out for hidden copies: building \u003ccode>path + [node.val]\u003c/code> at every step copies the path, costing O(n h) in total, and copying \u003ccode>path[:]\u003c/code> is only acceptable at the leaves where you actually store it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>The answer at a node depends on\u003c/th>\u003cth>Direction\u003c/th>\u003cth>Mechanism\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>its ancestors or the path from the root\u003c/td>\u003ctd>down\u003c/td>\u003ctd>argument\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>its descendants\u003c/td>\u003ctd>up\u003c/td>\u003ctd>return value\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>both\u003c/td>\u003ctd>both\u003c/td>\u003ctd>argument in, count or result out\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>a path anywhere in the tree\u003c/td>\u003ctd>up, plus a global best\u003c/td>\u003ctd>next card\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Cues: \"path from the root\", \"ancestor\", \"so far\", \"at depth\", \"root-to-leaf\". Not the right tool if a fact needs the whole subtree (sizes, heights): those must flow up, because a parent cannot know them before its children are processed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count good nodes (no ancestor has a larger value) in \u003ccode>[5, 3, 8, 6, 2, None, 7]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>          5\n         / \\\n        3   8\n       / \\    \\\n      6   2    7\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">walk(5, best=-inf): nothing is above the root, so 5 is good. Its children will be told best = 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  \u003cmark>walk(5, -inf)\u003c/mark>\ngood   5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">walk(3, best=5): 3 &lt; 5, not good. Children are told max(5, 3) = 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  walk(5, -inf) &gt; \u003cmark>walk(3, 5)\u003c/mark>\ngood   5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">walk(6, best=5): 6 &gt;= 5, good. Its empty children return 0, so this call returns 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  walk(5, -inf) &gt; walk(3, 5) &gt; \u003cmark>walk(6, 5)\u003c/mark>\ngood   5 \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">walk(2, best=5): 2 &lt; 5, not good; returns 0. walk(3) now returns 0 + 1 + 0 = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  walk(5, -inf) &gt; walk(3, 5) &gt; \u003cmark>walk(2, 5)\u003c/mark>\ngood   5 6\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">walk(8, best=5): good. Its child is told best = 8; note it was never told about 3 or 6, which are not its ancestors\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  walk(5, -inf) &gt; \u003cmark>walk(8, 5)\u003c/mark>\ngood   5 6 \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">walk(7, best=8): 7 &lt; 8, not good. walk(8) returns 1, and walk(5) returns 1 + 1 + 1 = 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  walk(5, -inf) &gt; walk(8, 5) &gt; \u003cmark>walk(7, 8)\u003c/mark>\ngood   5 6 8   (answer 3)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import Counter\n\n\ndef good_nodes(root):\n    \"\"\"Count nodes whose value is &gt;= every value on the path above them.\"\"\"\n    def walk(node, best):                     # best: largest value above node\n        if node is None:\n            return 0\n        good = 1 if node.val &gt;= best else 0\n        best = max(best, node.val)            # what the children are told\n        return good + walk(node.left, best) + walk(node.right, best)\n\n    return walk(root, float(\"-inf\"))\n\n\ndef count_paths_with_sum(root, target):\n    \"\"\"Number of downward paths (any start, any end) whose values sum to target.\"\"\"\n    seen = Counter({0: 1})                    # prefix sums on the current root path\n\n    def walk(node, running):\n        if node is None:\n            return 0\n        running += node.val\n        found = seen[running - target]        # paths that end at this node\n        seen[running] += 1\n        found += walk(node.left, running) + walk(node.right, running)\n        seen[running] -= 1                    # leaving this node: undo\n        return found\n\n    return walk(root, 0)\n\n\ndef root_to_leaf_paths(root):\n    \"\"\"Every root-to-leaf path as a list of values.\"\"\"\n    paths, path = [], []\n\n    def walk(node):\n        if node is None:\n            return\n        path.append(node.val)\n        if node.left is None and node.right is None:\n            paths.append(path[:])             # copy: path keeps changing\n        walk(node.left)\n        walk(node.right)\n        path.pop()                            # undo before returning\n\n    walk(root)\n    return paths\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In \u003ccode>count_paths_with_sum\u003c/code> the lookup happens before the current prefix is added, so a path is never empty, even when \u003ccode>target\u003c/code> is 0.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting to undo shared state\u003c/b>: without \u003ccode>path.pop()\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>seen[running] -= 1\u003c/code>, state from a finished branch leaks into its sibling.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting the seed\u003c/b>: without \u003ccode>Counter({0: 1})\u003c/code>, paths that start at the root are never counted.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Storing the live list\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>paths.append(path)\u003c/code> stores a reference, and every stored path ends up empty after the walk. Copy at the leaf.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Leaf tests\u003c/b>: a leaf has \u003ci>both\u003c/i> children None. \"Root-to-leaf\" problems go wrong when a node with one child is treated as a leaf, or when the empty tree is counted as a path with sum 0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Rebinding in a closure\u003c/b>: assigning to an outer variable inside the nested function needs \u003ccode>nonlocal\u003c/code>. Passing state as an argument avoids that and makes the undo automatic.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Trace \u003ccode>count_paths_with_sum\u003c/code> on the chain 1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3 (1 is the root, each node the left child of the previous) with target 3. What does \u003ccode>seen\u003c/code> contain at node 3, and what is the answer?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Running sums are 1, 3 and 6. At node 2 (running 3), \u003ccode>seen\u003c/code> holds {0: 1, 1: 1} and \u003ccode>seen[3 - 3] = seen[0] = 1\u003c/code>: the path 1, 2. At node 3 (running 6), \u003ccode>seen\u003c/code> holds {0: 1, 1: 1, 3: 1} and \u003ccode>seen[6 - 3] = 1\u003c/code>: the path 3 alone. The answer is 2.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why must \u003ccode>seen[running]\u003c/code> be decremented when a call returns? Give a tree where skipping it gives a wrong count.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The counter must describe only the ancestors of the current node; a prefix left behind by a finished branch belongs to a node that is not an ancestor, so pairing with it counts a \"path\" that climbs up and comes back down. Take \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3]\u003c/code> with target 1: the only answer is the root alone, so the count is 1. Without the undo, the prefix 3 from node 2 is still in \u003ccode>seen\u003c/code> when node 3 is visited (running sum 4), and \u003ccode>seen[4 - 1]\u003c/code> adds a bogus path, giving 2.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">For each, which direction does the information flow: (a) the depth of every node, (b) the number of leaves below each node, (c) whether each node's value equals the sum of all its ancestors?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(a) Down: depth comes from the parent's depth plus one. (b) Up: a node's leaf count is the sum of its children's. (c) Down: pass the ancestor sum as an argument and compare at each node.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Adapt the template to \"sum of all numbers spelled by root-to-leaf paths\", where the path 4, 9, 5 spells 495.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Pass \u003ccode>number = number * 10 + node.val\u003c/code> down. At a leaf, return that number; otherwise return the sum of the two child calls; an empty child returns 0. For \u003ccode>[3, 1, 4, 2, 5]\u003c/code> the paths spell 312, 315 and 34, so the answer is 661. One pass, O(n) time, O(h) space.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1448\u003c/b>: count good nodes with the \u003ccode>best\u003c/code> argument. Expected: 3 for \u003ccode>[5, 3, 8, 6, 2, None, 7]\u003c/code> (the nodes 5, 6 and 8), 1 for a single node, and 4 for \u003ccode>[2, 2, 2, 2]\u003c/code> (equal values count as good); O(n) time, O(h) space.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>437\u003c/b>: count downward paths with the prefix counter. Expected: for \u003ccode>[2, 1, 3, 1, None, -1, 4]\u003c/code>, target 4 gives 3 (the paths 2-1-1, 2-3-(-1) and 4) and target 3 gives 2 (2-1 and 3); O(n) time. Check it against a brute force that starts a walk from every node.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to pass path state down a tree, combine it with values returned up, and undo shared state so sibling branches never see each other.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:trees/how-do-you-find-the-best-path-when-it-does-not-h":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every path in a tree has exactly one \u003cb>highest node\u003c/b>, where it bends. So try every node as the bend: there, the best path is the best downward arm into the left subtree plus the node plus the best downward arm into the right subtree. Each call \u003cb>returns\u003c/b> only a one-armed path (the only shape its parent can extend) and \u003cb>records\u003c/b> the two-armed candidate in a global best. One post-order pass, O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Lay a string along the longest route through a tree of roads. Pick it up by its highest point and it hangs down in at most two strands, one into each side. Conversely, any node with its two longest strands hanging down is a candidate route. If every node reports \"the longest strand that hangs below me\" to its parent, each node can form its own candidate (left strand + right strand) and pass up only the longer strand plus itself, because a parent's route can continue through the node into one side only. A route that went into both sides and then up to the parent would visit the node twice: that is a fork, not a path.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Two numbers per node, with different jobs\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Quantity\u003c/th>\u003cth>Shape\u003c/th>\u003cth>Who uses it\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>returned value\u003c/td>\u003ctd>best path going \u003cb>down\u003c/b> from the node through at most one child\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the parent, to extend\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>candidate\u003c/td>\u003ctd>best path whose \u003cb>highest node\u003c/b> is this one, using up to two children\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the global answer only\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The answer is the maximum candidate over all nodes, and it is correct because the bend of the optimal path is some node, and at that node the candidate is exactly the optimal path (its two arms are the best arms, or the path could be improved).\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Diameter (longest path, counted in edges)\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Let the call return the height of the subtree in nodes (0 for empty). At a node with child heights L and R, the longest path bending here has L + R edges: L edges down into the left subtree to its deepest node, R into the right. Record \u003ccode>best = max(best, L + R)\u003c/code> and return \u003ccode>1 + max(L, R)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>On \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code>: the leaves return 1 and record 0; node 2 records 1 + 1 = 2 and returns 2; node 3 records 0 + 1 = 1 and returns 2; the root records 2 + 2 = 4. The diameter is 4, the path 4-2-1-3-6.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Maximum path sum (values can be negative)\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Now the arms have weights. Let \u003ccode>gain(node)\u003c/code> be the best sum of a downward path starting at node. An arm with negative gain should be dropped, so clip it: \u003ccode>l = max(gain(left), 0)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>r = max(gain(right), 0)\u003c/code>. Record \u003ccode>node.val + l + r\u003c/code> and return \u003ccode>node.val + max(l, r)\u003c/code>. The node itself is always included, so the answer for an all-negative tree is its largest single value, which is why \u003ccode>best\u003c/code> must start at negative infinity, not 0.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One post-order pass: each node does O(1) work after its two calls, so \u003cb>O(n) time\u003c/b> and \u003cb>O(h) stack\u003c/b>. The brute force tries every pair of nodes as endpoints, finds the path between them and sums it: O(n^2) pairs times O(h) per path. The bend argument collapses all pairs that share a highest node into one O(1) step at that node.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Cues: \"any path\", \"between any two nodes\", \"the path may or may not pass through the root\", \"longest\" or \"maximum\" over paths. The same split (return a one-sided summary, record a two-sided candidate) solves the longest path of equal values, the longest zigzag, and many tree DP problems; the advanced DP module extends it to nodes that return small tables (for example \"best with this node taken / not taken\").\u003c/p>\u003cp>If the path must start at the root or end at a leaf, you do not need the global: return values (or arguments passed down) are enough. If the question is about distances between many specified pairs of nodes, use lowest common ancestors instead (next card).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>gain\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>[-3, 4, 10, None, None, -6, 8]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>          -3\n         /  \\\n        4    10\n            /  \\\n          -6    8\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">gain(4): a leaf, both arms are 0. Candidate 4 + 0 + 0 = 4, so best = 4. Return 4 + max(0, 0) = 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>node   returns  candidate  best\n4      \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>        \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>          \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">gain(-6): a leaf with a negative value. Candidate -6 does not beat 4. Return -6 (the parent will clip it)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>node   returns  candidate  best\n4      4        4          4\n-6     \u003cmark>-6\u003c/mark>       \u003cmark>-6\u003c/mark>         4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">gain(8): a leaf. Candidate 8 beats 4, so best = 8. Return 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>node   returns  candidate  best\n-6     -6       -6         4\n8      \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>        \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>          \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">gain(10): l = max(-6, 0) = 0 (the negative arm is dropped), r = 8. Candidate 10 + 0 + 8 = 18, best = 18. Return 10 + max(0, 8) = 18\u003c/p>\u003cpre>node   returns  candidate  best\n8      8        8          8\n10     \u003cmark>18\u003c/mark>       \u003cmark>18\u003c/mark>         \u003cmark>18\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">gain(-3): l = 4, r = 18. Candidate -3 + 4 + 18 = 19, best = 19. It returns 15, which nobody uses\u003c/p>\u003cpre>node   returns  candidate  best\n10     18       18         18\n-3     \u003cmark>15\u003c/mark>       \u003cmark>19\u003c/mark>         \u003cmark>19\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The answer is best = 19: the path 4, -3, 10, 8 bends at the root. The -6 was left out because its arm was clipped to 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>answer \u003cmark>19\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def diameter(root):\n    \"\"\"Edges on the longest path between any two nodes.\"\"\"\n    best = 0\n\n    def height(node):                 # returns: nodes on the longest downward path\n        nonlocal best\n        if node is None:\n            return 0\n        left, right = height(node.left), height(node.right)\n        best = max(best, left + right)     # records: longest path bending here\n        return 1 + max(left, right)\n\n    height(root)\n    return best\n\n\ndef max_path_sum(root):\n    \"\"\"Largest sum of values on any non-empty path.\"\"\"\n    best = float(\"-inf\")\n\n    def gain(node):                   # returns: best downward path starting at node\n        nonlocal best\n        if node is None:\n            return 0\n        left = max(gain(node.left), 0)     # drop arms that only lose value\n        right = max(gain(node.right), 0)\n        best = max(best, node.val + left + right)   # records: best path bending here\n        return node.val + max(left, right)\n\n    gain(root)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>nonlocal best\u003c/code> lets the nested function rebind the outer variable. Without it, the assignment would make \u003ccode>best\u003c/code> local and the first read would raise \u003ccode>UnboundLocalError\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Returning the two-armed value\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>return node.val + left + right\u003c/code> lets a parent extend a path that already uses both children of the node: a fork. The answer comes out too large.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Starting best at 0\u003c/b> for path sums: on \u003ccode>[-3]\u003c/code> the answer is -3, not 0. Start at negative infinity (or \u003ccode>root.val\u003c/code>).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Not clipping negative arms\u003c/b>: then the path is forced to include a losing subtree. Clip with \u003ccode>max(gain, 0)\u003c/code>, but never clip the node's own value.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Edges versus nodes\u003c/b>: diameter is usually measured in edges. If your helper returns heights in nodes, \u003ccode>left + right\u003c/code> is already in edges; adding 1 counts nodes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>State that outlives the call\u003c/b>: a module-level or class-level \u003ccode>best\u003c/code> that is not reset carries the previous test case's answer into the next one. Keep it local to the outer function.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Run \u003ccode>diameter\u003c/code> on the chain \u003ccode>[1, 2, None, 3, None, 4]\u003c/code> (each node the left child of the previous). What does each call return and record?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Node 4 returns 1 and records 0; node 3 returns 2 and records 1 + 0 = 1; node 2 returns 3 and records 2; node 1 returns 4 and records 3. The diameter is 3 edges, from 1 to 4.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is it enough to consider, at each node, only paths whose highest node is that node?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every path has a unique node closest to the root (its highest node), because the path goes up to it and then down. So the set of all paths is partitioned by highest node, and taking the best candidate at each node and then the best over all nodes covers every path exactly once. At a fixed highest node the best path uses the best arm on each side, which the children's returned values provide.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A buggy version returns \u003ccode>node.val + left + right\u003c/code>. On \u003ccode>[1, 2, None, 3, 4]\u003c/code> (2 is the left child of 1, and 3 and 4 are the children of 2), what does it output, and what is correct?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The correct answer is 9, the path 3, 2, 4. The buggy \u003ccode>gain(2)\u003c/code> returns 2 + 3 + 4 = 9 to the root, which then records 1 + 9 + 0 = 10, a path that would have to visit 2 twice. It outputs 10.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Adapt the pattern to \"the longest path (in edges) on which every node has the same value\".\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Let the call return the longest one-armed same-value chain going down from the node, in edges. For each child, the arm is \u003ccode>child_result + 1\u003c/code> if the child exists and has the same value as the node, else 0. Record \u003ccode>left_arm + right_arm\u003c/code>, return \u003ccode>max(left_arm, right_arm)\u003c/code>. One O(n) pass, global best starting at 0.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>543\u003c/b>: diameter with the \u003ccode>height\u003c/code> helper. Expected: 4 for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code>, 3 for the chain \u003ccode>[1, 2, None, 3, None, 4]\u003c/code>, 0 for a single node; O(n) time, O(h) space.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>124\u003c/b>: maximum path sum with clipped arms. Expected: 19 for \u003ccode>[-3, 4, 10, None, None, -6, 8]\u003c/code>, -3 for \u003ccode>[-3]\u003c/code>, 9 for \u003ccode>[1, 2, None, 3, 4]\u003c/code>, and 2 for \u003ccode>[2, -1]\u003c/code>; O(n) time. Compare against an all-pairs brute force on random small trees with negative values.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to find the best path anywhere in a tree by returning one-armed answers and recording two-armed candidates at each node.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:trees/how-do-you-find-the-lowest-common-ancestor-of-tw":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The \u003cb>lowest common ancestor\u003c/b> (LCA) of p and q is the deepest node that has both in its subtree (a node counts as its own ancestor). One post-order pass finds it: each call returns p or q if it finds one in its subtree, the LCA if it finds both, and None otherwise; the first node that hears back from both sides is the answer. With parent pointers, walk up from p collecting ancestors, then walk up from q until you hit one.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Two employees want to know the most junior manager they both report to, directly or indirectly. One way: the first employee lists her whole management chain up to the CEO; the second walks up his chain until he meets a name on her list. That is the parent-pointer method.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The other way starts at the top: the CEO asks each division \"is either of them in your division?\" If both divisions say yes, the CEO is the answer. If only one says yes, the answer is inside that division, so the CEO simply passes its reply up. That is the recursive method, and it is just the bottom-up pattern with a clever return value.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>One pass from the root\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Contract: \u003ccode>lca(node)\u003c/code> returns p or q if exactly one of them is in node's subtree, the LCA if both are, and None if neither is (assuming both are in the tree). Cases at a node:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>node is None, p or q: return node. If node is p, any q below it would make p the LCA anyway, so there is no need to look further.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Ask both children.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Both answers non-None: p is on one side and q on the other, so this node is the lowest point that sees both: return it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Otherwise return whichever answer is non-None (or None). Once the true LCA is found, it is the only non-None value on its side all the way up, so it is passed to the root unchanged.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch5>Parent pointers\u003c/h5>\u003cp>If each node knows its parent (or you build a \u003ccode>parent\u003c/code> dictionary in one O(n) traversal), an LCA query needs no search from the root: collect p's ancestors in a set, then walk up from q until the current node is in the set. That is O(h) time per query and O(h) extra memory. Equalising depths first (move the deeper node up until both have the same depth, then move both up together until they meet) needs O(1) extra memory, exactly like finding where two linked lists merge.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>A tree is also a graph\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The parent map gives every node up to three neighbours: left, right and parent. With it, \"all nodes at distance k from a target\" is just breadth-first search from the target for k rounds, with a visited set so you do not walk back the way you came. \"Distance between p and q\" is depth(p) + depth(q) - 2 depth(lca).\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Many queries\u003c/h5>\u003cp>For 10^5 queries on a 10^5-node tree, neither method is fast enough per query on a deep tree. Binary lifting stores each node's 2^k-th ancestor for every k (O(n log n) preprocessing) and answers each query in O(log n). In a binary search tree you do not need any of this: the values tell you which side p and q are on (next module).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The recursive version visits each node at most once: \u003cb>O(n) time, O(h) stack\u003c/b> per query. Building a parent map is O(n) once; each parent-pointer query is then O(h). The distance-k search is O(n): the parent map plus a BFS that visits each node at most once.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Cues: \"lowest common ancestor\", \"common manager\", \"closest shared folder\", \"distance between two nodes\", \"path from one node to another\" (the path goes up to the LCA and down again), and version control: Git's merge base of two branches is a best common ancestor in the commit graph, computed so a three-way merge can compare both sides with their shared starting point.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Situation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Method\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>One query, child pointers only\u003c/td>\u003ctd>recursive pass\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Parent pointers available, few queries\u003c/td>\u003ctd>ancestor set or depth equalising\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(h) each\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Many queries on a static tree\u003c/td>\u003ctd>binary lifting\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n log n) build, O(log n) each\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Binary search tree\u003c/td>\u003ctd>follow the values from the root\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(h) each\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>lca(root, p=5, q=6)\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">lca(1): 1 is neither p nor q, so it asks its left child, which asks its left child: lca(4)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack     lca(1) &gt; lca(2) &gt; \u003cmark>lca(4)\u003c/mark>\nreturned  (none yet)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">lca(4): not p or q, and both of its children return None, so it returns None\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack     lca(1) &gt; lca(2)\nreturned  4 -&gt; \u003cmark>None\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">lca(5): 5 is p, so it returns itself at once without looking below\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack     lca(1) &gt; lca(2)\nreturned  4 -&gt; None, 5 -&gt; \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">lca(2): left None, right 5. Only one side found something, so 2 passes 5 up\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack     lca(1)\nreturned  4 -&gt; None, 5 -&gt; 5, 2 -&gt; \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">lca(3): its left child is None; lca(6) returns 6 because 6 is q. One side found something: 3 passes 6 up\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack     lca(1)\nreturned  2 -&gt; 5, 6 -&gt; 6, 3 -&gt; \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">lca(1): left returned 5 and right returned 6, so p and q are on different sides. The answer is 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack     (empty)\nreturned  2 -&gt; 5, 3 -&gt; 6, 1 -&gt; \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For p = 2 and q = 5 the walk stops at node 2 (it is p) and returns 2 without visiting 4 or 5; the right side returns None, so the answer is 2, which is correct because 5 is below 2.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\n\ndef lowest_common_ancestor(root, p, q):\n    \"\"\"LCA of nodes p and q, both assumed to be in the tree.\"\"\"\n    if root is None or root is p or root is q:\n        return root\n    left = lowest_common_ancestor(root.left, p, q)\n    right = lowest_common_ancestor(root.right, p, q)\n    if left and right:              # p and q are on different sides\n        return root\n    return left or right            # both on one side, or neither\n\n\ndef parent_map(root):\n    \"\"\"Map each node to its parent (the root maps to None).\"\"\"\n    parent, stack = {root: None}, [root]\n    while stack:\n        node = stack.pop()\n        for child in (node.left, node.right):\n            if child:\n                parent[child] = node\n                stack.append(child)\n    return parent\n\n\ndef lca_with_parents(p, q, parent):\n    ancestors = set()\n    while p:\n        ancestors.add(p)\n        p = parent[p]\n    while q not in ancestors:\n        q = parent[q]\n    return q\n\n\ndef nodes_at_distance(root, target, k):\n    \"\"\"Values of all nodes exactly k edges away from target.\"\"\"\n    parent = parent_map(root)\n    seen, frontier = {target}, deque([target])\n    for _ in range(k):\n        for _ in range(len(frontier)):\n            node = frontier.popleft()\n            for nxt in (node.left, node.right, parent[node]):\n                if nxt and nxt not in seen:\n                    seen.add(nxt)\n                    frontier.append(nxt)\n    return [node.val for node in frontier]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Nodes are compared with \u003ccode>is\u003c/code> and used as dictionary keys by identity (the default for objects without \u003ccode>__eq__\u003c/code>), so duplicate values are harmless.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>A node that is not in the tree\u003c/b>: the one-pass version assumes both exist. If q is missing, it returns p, which is wrong. When existence is not guaranteed, count how many of p and q were actually found during the walk.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Comparing values instead of nodes\u003c/b>: with duplicate values, \u003ccode>node.val == p.val\u003c/code> matches the wrong node. Compare identities.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Many queries, one pass each\u003c/b>: 10^5 queries of O(n) each is 10^10 steps. Switch to parent pointers with depths, or binary lifting.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Walking back into visited nodes\u003c/b>: in the distance-k search the parent link makes the tree an undirected graph, so a visited set is mandatory, or the search bounces between a node and its parent.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deep trees\u003c/b>: on a chain, the recursive version hits Python's recursion limit; build the parent map iteratively (as \u003ccode>parent_map\u003c/code> does) and use the parent-pointer query.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">On \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code>, what does \u003ccode>lca(2)\u003c/code> return when p = 4 and q = 6, and what is the final answer?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>lca(2)\u003c/code> returns 4: its left call finds 4 (which is p) and its right call (node 5) returns None. \u003ccode>lca(3)\u003c/code> returns 6. The root receives 4 from the left and 6 from the right, so the answer is 1.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is it safe to return p immediately when the walk reaches p, without checking whether q is below it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Either q is below p, in which case p is the LCA and returning it is right, or q is elsewhere, in which case some ancestor of p will also receive q from its other side and become the answer, and p's reply only says \"one of them is here\". In both cases the contract (return p, q or the LCA, whichever applies) is satisfied, and the guarantee that both nodes exist removes the need to search further.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you compute the number of edges between nodes p and q in a tree you will query 10^5 times?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Precompute every node's depth and parent once (O(n)), then answer each query as depth(p) + depth(q) - 2 * depth(lca(p, q)). For deep trees, use binary lifting so each LCA costs O(log n) instead of O(h).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A function finds all nodes at distance k from a target with BFS over left, right and parent, but without a visited set. What goes wrong for target 2, k = 2 on \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>In round 1 it reaches 4, 5 and 1. In round 2, 4 and 5 step back to their parent 2 (twice), and 1 steps to 2 and to 3. The answer comes out as 2, 2, 2 and 3 instead of just 3: the target itself is reported several times because the search walked back the way it came.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>236\u003c/b>: one-pass LCA. Expected on \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code>: p = 5, q = 6 gives 1; p = 2, q = 5 gives 2; p = 4, q = 5 gives 2; O(n) time, O(h) space. Check it against a brute force that compares the two root-to-node paths.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>863\u003c/b>: parent map plus BFS. Expected on the same tree with target 2: k = 1 gives 4, 5 and 1 (any order); k = 2 gives 3; k = 3 gives 6; k = 4 gives an empty list; O(n) time and space.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to find lowest common ancestors with one pass or parent pointers, and how a parent map turns a tree into a graph you can search in any direction.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:trees/how-do-you-rebuild-a-tree-from-its-traversals-an":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A traversal list alone loses the shape, but two can restore it: \u003cb>pre-order names the root, in-order splits left from right\u003c/b>, so with distinct values pre-order plus in-order determines the tree. To store a tree, one pre-order walk is enough if you also write a \u003cb>marker for every missing child\u003c/b>; reading the tokens back in the same order rebuilds it. Both directions are O(n) with an index map and an iterator.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The list \u003ccode>1, 2\u003c/code> in pre-order could be a root 1 with 2 on the left or on the right; the shape is gone. In-order alone fails too: \u003ccode>2, 1\u003c/code> could be 2 above 1 or 1 above 2. Each list keeps a different half of the information. Pre-order tells you, at every step, \u003ci>which node is the root\u003c/i> of the subtree being built; in-order tells you \u003ci>how many nodes fall on each side\u003c/i> of it. Together they leave no choice.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Serialisation solves the same problem differently: instead of a second list, write down the gaps. In \u003ccode>1,2,#,#,3,#,#\u003c/code>, after reading 2 you know it is a leaf (two markers follow), so 3 must be the root's right child. HTML does the same with closing tags: an element's opening tag is written at its pre-order moment and its closing tag at its post-order moment, which is why a page can be parsed back into the same DOM tree.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Pre-order plus in-order\u003c/h5>\u003col>\u003cli>Map each value to its in-order index (values must be distinct).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Consume pre-order left to right with an iterator. The next value is the root of the subtree you are building.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Build the subtree whose values are \u003ccode>inorder[lo:hi]\u003c/code>: take the next pre-order value as root, find its index \u003ccode>mid\u003c/code>, build the left subtree from \u003ccode>inorder[lo:mid]\u003c/code>, then the right from \u003ccode>inorder[mid + 1:hi]\u003c/code>. Left before right, because that is the order pre-order lists them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An empty range means an empty subtree.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Post-order plus in-order works the same way from the other end: consume post-order backwards (root last) and build the \u003cb>right\u003c/b> subtree first. Pre-order plus post-order is \u003cb>not\u003c/b> enough in general: a root 1 with a single child 2 has pre-order \u003ccode>1, 2\u003c/code> and post-order \u003ccode>2, 1\u003c/code> whether 2 is the left or the right child. It is unique only for full binary trees (every node has 0 or 2 children).\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Serialise with markers\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Pre-order, writing \u003ccode>#\u003c/code> for every empty child. Deserialise with the same recursion: read a token; \u003ccode>#\u003c/code> means None; otherwise make a node and read its left subtree, then its right subtree, from the same token stream. Correctness is an induction on the token list: the serialisation of a subtree is a contiguous block that the reader consumes exactly, so after reading the left subtree the stream is positioned at the start of the right one.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Level order with \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> gaps (the LeetCode format from the BFS card) is an equally valid serialisation. It is more readable for wide shallow trees and avoids deep recursion; pre-order with markers streams naturally and is easier to code.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Using serialisation for comparison\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Two subtrees are identical exactly when their marker serialisations are equal strings, so serialising every subtree in post-order and counting the strings in a dictionary finds duplicate subtrees. Strings get long, so production versions map each distinct subtree to a small integer id and serialise as \u003ccode>(left id, value, right id)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>With the index map, each node is created once with an O(1) lookup: \u003cb>O(n) time\u003c/b>, O(n) for the map, O(h) stack. The textbook version that slices lists and calls \u003ccode>inorder.index(root)\u003c/code> at every level is O(n^2) on a chain: each level searches and copies an O(n) list.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Serialising writes 2n + 1 tokens (n values and n + 1 markers), O(n) time with \u003ccode>\",\".join\u003c/code>. Deserialising reads each token once: O(n).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The input is given as traversals (rebuild), or you must store, send, cache or hash a tree (serialise).\u003c/li>\u003cli>You need to compare or deduplicate subtrees (serialise each one, or give each an id).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Copying a tree: a pre-order walk that creates each node before its children.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Do not rebuild from pre-order and post-order unless the tree is guaranteed full, and do not use the index map when values repeat: then the split point is ambiguous and the input may not determine a unique tree at all.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Rebuild from pre-order \u003ccode>[1, 2, 4, 5, 3, 6]\u003c/code> and in-order \u003ccode>[4, 2, 5, 1, 3, 6]\u003c/code>. Brackets mark the in-order range of the subtree being built:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">build(0, 6): the next pre-order value 1 is the root. Its in-order index is 3, so [4 2 5] is its left subtree and [3 6] its right\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pre   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark> 2 4 5 3 6\nin    [4 2 5] \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark> [3 6]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">build(0, 3): the next pre-order value 2 roots the left subtree. Index 1 splits the range into [4] and [5]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pre   1 \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark> 4 5 3 6\nin    [4] \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark> [5] 1 3 6\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">build(0, 1): the next value 4 has index 0. Both sub-ranges are empty, so 4 is a leaf\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pre   1 2 \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark> 5 3 6\nin    \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark> 2 5 1 3 6\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">build(2, 3): the next value 5 is also a leaf. The left subtree of 1 is complete\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pre   1 2 4 \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark> 3 6\nin    4 2 \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark> 1 3 6\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">build(4, 6): the next value 3 roots the right subtree. Index 4: its left range is empty, its right range is [6]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pre   1 2 4 5 \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark> 6\nin    4 2 5 1 \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark> [6]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">build(5, 6): 6 is a leaf. Every pre-order value was consumed exactly once, and the tree is [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pre   1 2 4 5 3 \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>\nin    4 2 5 1 3 \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def build_from_pre_in(preorder, inorder):\n    \"\"\"Rebuild a tree with distinct values from its pre-order and in-order lists.\"\"\"\n    where = {v: i for i, v in enumerate(inorder)}\n    next_root = iter(preorder)\n\n    def build(lo, hi):                  # the subtree whose values are inorder[lo:hi]\n        if lo &gt;= hi:\n            return None\n        node = TreeNode(next(next_root))\n        mid = where[node.val]\n        node.left = build(lo, mid)      # left first: pre-order lists it first\n        node.right = build(mid + 1, hi)\n        return node\n\n    return build(0, len(inorder))\n\n\ndef serialise(root):\n    \"\"\"Pre-order with '#' for every missing child, e.g. '1,2,#,#,3,#,#'.\"\"\"\n    out = []\n\n    def walk(node):\n        if node is None:\n            out.append(\"#\")\n            return\n        out.append(str(node.val))\n        walk(node.left)\n        walk(node.right)\n\n    walk(root)\n    return \",\".join(out)\n\n\ndef deserialise(data):\n    \"\"\"Inverse of serialise.\"\"\"\n    tokens = iter(data.split(\",\"))\n\n    def walk():\n        token = next(tokens)\n        if token == \"#\":\n            return None\n        node = TreeNode(int(token))\n        node.left = walk()\n        node.right = walk()\n        return node\n\n    return walk()\n\n\ntree = build_from_pre_in([1, 2, 4, 5, 3, 6], [4, 2, 5, 1, 3, 6])\nprint(serialise(tree))                    # 1,2,4,#,#,5,#,#,3,#,6,#,#\nprint(serialise(deserialise(\"#\")))        # #\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Right before left\u003c/b>: with pre-order, building \u003ccode>node.right\u003c/code> first assigns the left subtree's values to the right side. (With post-order consumed backwards, it is the other way round.)\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Duplicate values\u003c/b> break the index map silently: the tree comes back with the wrong shape and no error.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No separator\u003c/b>: concatenating \u003ccode>\"1\" + \"23\"\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>\"12\" + \"3\"\u003c/code> gives the same string. Always separate tokens; a comma also keeps negative numbers like \u003ccode>-7\u003c/code> intact.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No markers\u003c/b>: a pre-order list without markers cannot be deserialised; you would need a second traversal.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Slicing\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>build(preorder[1:k], inorder[:m])\u003c/code> style code is easy to write and O(n^2) on skewed trees. Pass indices.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deep inputs\u003c/b>: both recursions are as deep as the tree. For long chains, serialise in level order with a queue or use an explicit stack.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Draw the tree for pre-order \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3]\u003c/code> and in-order \u003ccode>[2, 1, 3]\u003c/code>, and write its serialisation.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>1 is the root; in in-order, 2 is to its left and 3 to its right, so both are leaves: \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3]\u003c/code>. The serialisation is \u003ccode>1,2,#,#,3,#,#\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does reading the tokens in pre-order rebuild exactly the original tree?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>By induction on the tree: the serialisation of an empty tree is the single token \u003ccode>#\u003c/code>, and that of a node is its value, then its left subtree's serialisation, then its right subtree's. The reader mirrors this: it reads a value, then calls itself for the left subtree, which (by the induction hypothesis) consumes exactly the left block and returns that subtree, leaving the stream at the start of the right block. So each recursive call consumes precisely its own block.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Give two different trees with the same pre-order and the same post-order.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Root 1 with only a left child 2, and root 1 with only a right child 2. Both have pre-order \u003ccode>1, 2\u003c/code> and post-order \u003ccode>2, 1\u003c/code>. Pre-order and post-order never reveal on which side a single child hangs.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Adapt \u003ccode>build_from_pre_in\u003c/code> to post-order plus in-order.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Iterate post-order from the end (\u003ccode>iter(reversed(postorder))\u003c/code>), because the root comes last; build \u003ccode>node.right = build(mid + 1, hi)\u003c/code> before \u003ccode>node.left = build(lo, mid)\u003c/code>, because reading post-order backwards yields root, then the right subtree, then the left. Everything else is unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>105\u003c/b>: rebuild with the index map and an iterator. Expected: pre-order \u003ccode>[1, 2, 4, 5, 3, 6]\u003c/code> with in-order \u003ccode>[4, 2, 5, 1, 3, 6]\u003c/code> gives \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code> in level order; a single value gives a single node; O(n) time. Test it by generating random trees with distinct values, computing their two traversals, rebuilding, and comparing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>297\u003c/b>: serialise and deserialise. Expected: \u003ccode>serialise\u003c/code> of \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>1,2,4,#,#,5,#,#,3,#,6,#,#\u003c/code>, the empty tree is \u003ccode>#\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>deserialise(serialise(t))\u003c/code> equals \u003ccode>t\u003c/code> for random trees, including negative and repeated values.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to rebuild a tree from pre-order and in-order lists in O(n), which traversal pairs are ambiguous, and how to serialise any tree with markers.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:trees/how-do-you-traverse-a-tree-without-recursion-and":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Recursion keeps its pending work on the call stack; you can keep the same work on a Python list instead. In-order: push the whole left spine, pop a node, emit it, then move to its right child. Pre-order: pop, emit, push right then left. Post-order: push each node twice, once to expand and once to emit. Same O(n) time and O(h) memory, but no recursion limit, and the walk can pause between values.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>When you read a book with footnotes inside footnotes, you keep a finger in each page you left. The fingers are a stack: the most recent one is where you return to first. Recursion keeps those fingers for you, in interpreter frames. An explicit stack keeps them in a list you control. Nothing else changes: the order of work is identical if you push things in the right order.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Why bother? In CPython each recursive call uses a frame, and the interpreter stops at about 1000 nested calls (\u003ccode>sys.getrecursionlimit()\u003c/code> returns 1000 by default). A tree built by inserting sorted keys into a plain search tree, or any \"linked list shaped\" input, can be 10^4 to 10^5 levels deep. \u003ccode>sys.setrecursionlimit\u003c/code> raises the ceiling, but the real C stack may overflow first and kill the process without a Python exception. A list on the heap has no such limit.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Pre-order: the easy one\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Pop a node, emit it, push its right child and then its left child. The left child is on top, so it is processed next, and the right child waits underneath until the whole left subtree is done: exactly the recursive order.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>In-order: walk the left spine\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The recursive in-order cannot emit a node until its left subtree is finished. Simulate that:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>From the current node, go left as far as possible, pushing every node on the way. These are nodes whose left subtrees are in progress.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Pop the top. Its left subtree is finished, so emit it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Move to its right child and repeat from step 1.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>The loop runs while there is a current node or something on the stack. \u003cb>Invariant\u003c/b>: the stack holds exactly the ancestors of the current position whose own value has not been emitted yet, deepest on top.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Post-order: push twice\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A node must be emitted after both subtrees. Push \u003ccode>(node, False)\u003c/code> meaning \"expand me\". When popped, push \u003ccode>(node, True)\u003c/code> (\"emit me\") first, then the right child, then the left child, so they come off the stack left, right, then the node itself. This \"push the steps in reverse\" recipe produces any order: move the \u003ccode>(node, True)\u003c/code> push to change pre-, in- or post-order.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A popular shortcut computes the modified pre-order root, right, left and reverses the output. It gives the right list, but only at the end; it cannot emit nodes one by one in post-order, which matters for iterators and for freeing memory as you go.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Iterators that pause\u003c/h5>\u003cp>An explicit stack also lets a traversal stop in the middle and resume later. A generator with the in-order loop above yields one value at a time, and the next module's search-tree iterator is exactly this stack stored in an object. (A recursive generator with \u003ccode>yield from\u003c/code> also pauses, but every level of nesting is still a live frame, so it hits the same recursion limit.)\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every node is pushed and popped once (twice in the post-order version), with O(1) work each: \u003cb>O(n) time\u003c/b>. The stack holds at most one entry per level for pre-order and in-order, plus the pending right children or markers, which is \u003cb>O(h)\u003c/b>: O(log n) for balanced trees and O(n) for chains, the same as recursion but on the heap.\u003c/p>\u003cp>In CPython the iterative and recursive versions are within a small constant factor of each other: a function call is relatively expensive, but so are the extra list operations and loop tests. Choose for depth and clarity, not for speed. Morris traversal (linked in the Go deeper notes) brings memory to O(1) by temporarily threading right pointers back to ancestors, at the cost of modifying the tree during the walk.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Situation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Choose\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>The tree may be deeper than about 1000 levels in Python\u003c/td>\u003ctd>explicit stack (or BFS if order does not matter)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>You need to pause and resume (iterators, k-th element, early exit)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>explicit stack\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Balanced or small trees, clarity matters most\u003c/td>\u003ctd>recursion\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>O(1) extra memory, and you may modify the tree temporarily\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Morris traversal\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Tree problems on LeetCode often allow 10^4 nodes, and a skewed tree of that size is ten times deeper than CPython's default limit, so know the stack version even if a judge happens to raise the limit for you. In production code that walks user-supplied data (JSON, file systems, syntax trees), prefer the stack: CPython's own \u003ccode>os.walk\u003c/code> switched to an explicit stack in Python 3.12.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Iterative in-order on \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">node = 1. Walk down the left spine pushing 1, 2 and 4; then node is None\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  [1, 2, \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>]\nout    []\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop 4, the leftmost unvisited node, and emit it. Its right child is None, so nothing is pushed\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  [1, 2]\nout    [\u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop 2 and emit it: its left subtree is finished. Move to its right child 5 and push its left spine (just 5)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  [1, \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>]\nout    [4, \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop 5 and emit it. It has no right child\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  [1]\nout    [4, 2, \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop 1 and emit it. Move to its right child 3 and push it (3 has no left child)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  [\u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>]\nout    [4, 2, 5, \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop 3, emit it, move to its right child 6 and push it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  [\u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>]\nout    [4, 2, 5, 1, \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop 6 and emit it. The stack is empty and node is None, so the walk ends. The stack never held more than 3 nodes, the height\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  []\nout    [4, 2, 5, 1, 3, \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def inorder_iterative(root):\n    out, stack, node = [], [], root\n    while stack or node:\n        while node:                  # push the left spine\n            stack.append(node)\n            node = node.left\n        node = stack.pop()           # leftmost node not yet emitted\n        out.append(node.val)\n        node = node.right            # then its right subtree\n    return out\n\n\ndef preorder_iterative(root):\n    out = []\n    stack = [root] if root else []\n    while stack:\n        node = stack.pop()\n        out.append(node.val)\n        if node.right:               # pushed first, so popped after the left\n            stack.append(node.right)\n        if node.left:\n            stack.append(node.left)\n    return out\n\n\ndef postorder_iterative(root):\n    \"\"\"Each node is pushed twice: once to expand, once to emit after its children.\"\"\"\n    out, stack = [], [(root, False)]\n    while stack:\n        node, expanded = stack.pop()\n        if node is None:\n            continue\n        if expanded:\n            out.append(node.val)\n        else:                        # push in reverse of the order wanted\n            stack.append((node, True))\n            stack.append((node.right, False))\n            stack.append((node.left, False))\n    return out\n\n\ndef max_depth_iterative(root):\n    best = 0\n    stack = [(root, 1)] if root else []\n    while stack:\n        node, depth = stack.pop()\n        best = max(best, depth)\n        for child in (node.left, node.right):\n            if child:\n                stack.append((child, depth + 1))\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Loop condition\u003c/b>: in-order needs \u003ccode>while stack or node\u003c/code>. With only \u003ccode>while stack\u003c/code>, the loop never starts (the stack is empty at first); with only \u003ccode>while node\u003c/code>, it stops at the first node without a right child.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Push order\u003c/b>: pushing left before right in pre-order yields root, right, left.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Raising the recursion limit as the fix\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>sys.setrecursionlimit(10**6)\u003c/code> silences the Python check, but a deep enough recursion then overflows the C stack and the process dies with a segmentation fault. If you must, also run the work in a thread with a larger stack (\u003ccode>threading.stack_size\u003c/code>); the explicit stack is simpler.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reversed pre-order as post-order\u003c/b>: fine for producing a list, wrong when you need to act on each node at its post-order moment (freeing, streaming).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Changing the tree during the walk\u003c/b>: if your loop body reassigns \u003ccode>node.right\u003c/code>, the walk follows the new pointer. Save what you need before changing links.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In the in-order trace above, what does the stack hold just after 5 is emitted, and what is emitted next?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Just \u003ccode>[1]\u003c/code>: node 1 is the only ancestor whose value is still pending. Since 5 has no right child, the next step pops 1 and emits it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does the in-order stack never hold more than h nodes?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every node on the stack is an ancestor of the current position whose value has not been emitted, and those ancestors lie on one root-to-node path, which has at most h nodes. A node is pushed when the walk passes it going left and popped before the walk moves into its right subtree, so no node from a finished subtree remains.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A service receives a binary tree as JSON-like nested objects, up to 200,000 nodes deep in the worst case, and must return its post-order. Recursion, raised limit, or explicit stack?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The explicit stack. Plain recursion raises RecursionError near depth 1000; raising the limit to 200,000 risks a hard crash from C stack overflow; the two-push post-order on a Python list handles any depth and keeps O(n) time.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">This pre-order loop prints \u003ccode>[1, 3, 2]\u003c/code> for the tree \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3]\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>node = stack.pop(); out.append(node.val); stack.append(node.left); stack.append(node.right)\u003c/code> (with a None check). What is wrong?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The left child is pushed first, so it ends up below the right child and is popped after it: the walk visits root, right, left. Push the right child first, then the left.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>94\u003c/b>: solve in-order again with \u003ccode>inorder_iterative\u003c/code>. Expected: \u003ccode>[4, 2, 5, 1, 3, 6]\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, None, 6]\u003c/code>. Then build a left chain of 100,000 nodes with a loop (values 1 to 100,000 going down) and run both versions: the iterative one returns 100,000 values starting with 100000; the recursive one raises \u003ccode>RecursionError\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>104\u003c/b>: solve maximum depth with \u003ccode>max_depth_iterative\u003c/code>. Expected: 3 for the example tree, and 100000 for the same chain, with no recursion at all.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to run pre-, in- and post-order traversals with an explicit stack, and when Python's recursion limit makes that necessary.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:trees/where-do-trees-run-in-real-software-and-which-tr":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Hierarchical data is a tree, and real tools pick traversals for the same reasons you do: \u003cb>pre-order\u003c/b> when a parent must come first (listing a folder before its contents, document order in the DOM), \u003cb>post-order\u003c/b> when a parent needs its children's results (folder sizes, deleting a directory, evaluating an expression, generating code), and \u003cb>breadth-first\u003c/b> when order does not matter or depth must stay bounded. Production code also guards against depth: explicit stacks and nesting limits.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Open a file manager: folders contain folders and files. Open a browser's developer tools: elements contain elements. Look at how Python sees \u003ccode>2 * (3 + 4) - 5\u003c/code>: an operator node whose children are smaller expressions. A JSON config is dictionaries inside lists inside dictionaries. Each is a rooted tree with ordered children, and every operation you run on them is one of this module's traversals.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>File systems\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Directories are internal nodes and files are leaves. (Hard links and symbolic links can make it a graph; \u003ccode>os.walk\u003c/code> does not follow symlinks unless \u003ccode>followlinks=True\u003c/code>, which avoids cycles.) \u003ccode>os.walk(top)\u003c/code> yields \u003ccode>(dirpath, dirnames, filenames)\u003c/code> for a directory \u003cb>before\u003c/b> its subdirectories by default (\u003ccode>topdown=True\u003c/code>, pre-order), and you can prune the walk by removing names from \u003ccode>dirnames\u003c/code> in place. With \u003ccode>topdown=False\u003c/code> it yields a directory \u003cb>after\u003c/b> all its subdirectories (post-order), which is what you need to delete a tree (a directory must be empty before \u003ccode>rmdir\u003c/code>) or to total sizes like \u003ccode>du\u003c/code>. In CPython 3.12, \u003ccode>os.walk\u003c/code> is implemented with an explicit stack rather than recursion, so very deep directory trees do not hit the recursion limit.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>The DOM\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A web page is a tree of elements. The DOM Standard defines \u003cb>tree order\u003c/b> as a pre-order, depth-first traversal, and APIs such as \u003ccode>querySelectorAll\u003c/code> return matches in that order. Serialising a page back to HTML writes each opening tag at the element's pre-order moment and each closing tag at its post-order moment, which is why the text can be parsed back into the same tree.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Compilers and abstract syntax trees\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003ccode>ast.parse(\"2 * (3 + 4) - 5\")\u003c/code> returns a tree: \u003ccode>BinOp(-)\u003c/code> with children \u003ccode>BinOp(*)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Constant(5)\u003c/code>, and so on. CPython's compiler emits bytecode in \u003cb>post-order\u003c/b>: operands before their operator. \u003ccode>dis.dis\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>a + b * c\u003c/code> shows \u003ccode>LOAD_NAME a\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>LOAD_NAME b\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>LOAD_NAME c\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>BINARY_OP *\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>BINARY_OP +\u003c/code>. The virtual machine then evaluates with a value stack, exactly like evaluating postfix (reverse Polish) notation. Python's \u003ccode>ast.NodeVisitor\u003c/code> walks depth-first with one \u003ccode>visit_&lt;Type&gt;\u003c/code> method per node type, while \u003ccode>ast.walk\u003c/code> is documented as \"in no specified order\" (in CPython it uses a deque, so it is breadth-first).\u003c/p>\u003ch5>JSON and other nested documents\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Parsed JSON is a tree of dicts and lists. Parsers recurse on nesting, so deeply nested input is a classic denial-of-service vector: CPython's \u003ccode>json.loads\u003c/code> raises \u003ccode>RecursionError\u003c/code> on 100,000 nested arrays. Services that accept untrusted documents either cap the depth before parsing or traverse with an explicit stack.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>All of these traversals are O(n) in the number of nodes, but the constant differs wildly: a DOM or AST walk touches objects in memory, while \u003ccode>os.walk\u003c/code> makes one system call per directory (a \u003ccode>scandir\u003c/code>), so file-system traversals are I/O-bound and the order you visit directories can matter more than the algorithm. Memory follows the traversal: pre-order and post-order walks hold one path (O(h)), breadth-first walks hold a level (O(w)).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>System\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tree\u003c/th>\u003cth>Traversal\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>os.walk(topdown=True)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>directories\u003c/td>\u003ctd>pre-order\u003c/td>\u003ctd>list or prune a folder before entering it\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>os.walk(topdown=False)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>rm -r\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>du\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>directories\u003c/td>\u003ctd>post-order\u003c/td>\u003ctd>children must be deleted or totalled first\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DOM \u003ccode>querySelectorAll\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>elements\u003c/td>\u003ctd>pre-order\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"document order\" is defined as pre-order\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Compiling or evaluating an expression\u003c/td>\u003ctd>AST\u003c/td>\u003ctd>post-order\u003c/td>\u003ctd>operands before operators\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Copying or serialising a document\u003c/td>\u003ctd>any\u003c/td>\u003ctd>pre-order\u003c/td>\u003ctd>create the parent before its children\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Checking a document's depth or size cheaply\u003c/td>\u003ctd>any\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS or explicit stack\u003c/td>\u003ctd>no recursion limit, bounded memory per level\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Evaluating \u003ccode>2 * (3 + 4) - 5\u003c/code> in post-order with a value stack (this is what the bytecode does):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>          -\n        /   \\\n       *     5\n      / \\\n     2   +\n        / \\\n       3   4\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Post-order visits the leaf 2 first: a constant, so push its value\u003c/p>\u003cpre>visit  \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark> 3 4 + * 5 -\nstack  [\u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The constants 3 and 4 come next: push both\u003c/p>\u003cpre>visit  2 \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark> \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark> + * 5 -\nstack  [2, \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">+ is visited after both of its children: pop 4 and 3, push 3 + 4 = 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>visit  2 3 4 \u003cmark>+\u003c/mark> * 5 -\nstack  [2, \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">* comes after its children: pop 7 and 2, push 2 * 7 = 14\u003c/p>\u003cpre>visit  2 3 4 + \u003cmark>*\u003c/mark> 5 -\nstack  [\u003cmark>14\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Push the constant 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>visit  2 3 4 + * \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark> -\nstack  [14, \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The root - is visited last: pop 5 and 14, push 14 - 5 = 9, the value of the expression\u003c/p>\u003cpre>visit  2 3 4 + * 5 \u003cmark>-\u003c/mark>\nstack  [\u003cmark>9\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import ast\nimport operator\nimport os\n\nOPS = {ast.Add: operator.add, ast.Sub: operator.sub,\n       ast.Mult: operator.mul, ast.Div: operator.truediv}\n\n\ndef evaluate(node):\n    \"\"\"Evaluate an arithmetic syntax tree in post-order.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Expression):\n        return evaluate(node.body)\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Constant):\n        return node.value\n    if isinstance(node, ast.BinOp):\n        left, right = evaluate(node.left), evaluate(node.right)   # children first\n        return OPS[type(node.op)](left, right)\n    raise ValueError(f\"unsupported node: {type(node).__name__}\")\n\n\ndef dir_sizes(top):\n    \"\"\"Total bytes of the files under every directory, computed bottom-up.\"\"\"\n    total = {}\n    for path, dirs, files in os.walk(top, topdown=False):\n        size = sum(os.path.getsize(os.path.join(path, f)) for f in files)\n        size += sum(total.get(os.path.join(path, d), 0) for d in dirs)\n        total[path] = size\n    return total\n\n\ndef json_depth(value):\n    \"\"\"Nesting depth of dicts and lists, without recursion (a scalar has depth 0).\"\"\"\n    best, stack = 0, [(value, 0)]\n    while stack:\n        item, depth = stack.pop()\n        if isinstance(item, dict):\n            item = list(item.values())\n        if isinstance(item, list):\n            best = max(best, depth + 1)\n            stack.extend((child, depth + 1) for child in item)\n    return best\n\n\nprint(evaluate(ast.parse(\"2 * (3 + 4) - 5\", mode=\"eval\")))   # 9\nprint(json_depth({\"a\": [1, {\"b\": []}]}))                    # 4\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>total.get(..., 0)\u003c/code> covers subdirectory entries that \u003ccode>os.walk\u003c/code> lists but does not enter, such as symbolic links to directories.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cycles in \"trees\"\u003c/b>: symlinks, hard links and object graphs with back-references turn trees into graphs. Keep \u003ccode>followlinks=False\u003c/code> or track visited identities.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pruning needs top-down\u003c/b>: editing \u003ccode>dirnames\u003c/code> in place only affects the walk when \u003ccode>topdown=True\u003c/code>; in post-order the subdirectories were already visited.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Errors are silent\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>os.walk\u003c/code> ignores errors such as permission denied unless you pass \u003ccode>onerror\u003c/code>. A size total can be quietly incomplete.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unbounded depth on untrusted input\u003c/b>: a recursive validator or pretty-printer is a crash waiting for a deep payload. Cap the depth or use a stack.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Relying on unspecified order\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>ast.walk\u003c/code> promises no order; if you need parents before children, use a \u003ccode>NodeVisitor\u003c/code> or your own traversal.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In what order does CPython emit the operations for \u003ccode>(a - b) * c\u003c/code>, and which traversal is that?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>LOAD_NAME a\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>LOAD_NAME b\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>BINARY_OP -\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>LOAD_NAME c\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>BINARY_OP *\u003c/code>: the subtraction's operands, the subtraction, then the other operand, then the multiplication. Operators come after their operands: post-order.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why must a recursive delete of a directory be post-order?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The operating system removes only empty directories, so every file and subdirectory inside must be gone before the directory itself. Post-order handles a node after all its children, which guarantees that.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your API accepts JSON configs and validates them with a recursive function. A user sends a 50,000-deep nested list. What happens, and what are two fixes?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Parsing (\u003ccode>json.loads\u003c/code>) or the recursive validator raises \u003ccode>RecursionError\u003c/code> (or, with a raised limit, may crash the process). Fixes: reject documents above a maximum size or nesting depth before full processing, and write the validator with an explicit stack, as \u003ccode>json_depth\u003c/code> does.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You call \u003ccode>os.walk(top)\u003c/code> and want to skip every \u003ccode>.git\u003c/code> folder and everything inside it. What do you do, and why does it require the default \u003ccode>topdown=True\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Inside the loop, remove it from the list in place: \u003ccode>dirs[:] = [d for d in dirs if d != \".git\"]\u003c/code>. In top-down mode \u003ccode>os.walk\u003c/code> decides which subdirectories to enter after yielding the parent, using that same list, so the pruned folders are never visited. In bottom-up mode the subdirectories have already been walked by the time the parent is yielded.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evaluate a syntax tree\u003c/b>: extend \u003ccode>evaluate\u003c/code> with unary minus (\u003ccode>ast.UnaryOp\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>ast.USub\u003c/code>) and \u003ccode>ast.Pow\u003c/code>. Expected: \u003ccode>2 * (3 + 4) - 5\u003c/code> gives 9, \u003ccode>-(2 ** 3) + 10\u003c/code> gives 2, and \u003ccode>print(ast.dump(ast.parse(\"1 + 2\", mode=\"eval\"), indent=2))\u003c/code> shows a \u003ccode>BinOp\u003c/code> whose children are two \u003ccode>Constant\u003c/code> nodes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Folder sizes\u003c/b>: in a scratch folder, create \u003ccode>demo/a/x.txt\u003c/code> with 10 bytes and \u003ccode>demo/a/b/y.txt\u003c/code> with 20 bytes, then call \u003ccode>dir_sizes(\"demo\")\u003c/code>. Expected: 20 for \u003ccode>demo/a/b\u003c/code>, 30 for \u003ccode>demo/a\u003c/code>, 30 for \u003ccode>demo\u003c/code> (paths printed with your platform's separator). Then switch to \u003ccode>topdown=True\u003c/code> and explain why the totals break.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deep JSON\u003c/b>: build \u003ccode>doc = []\u003c/code> nested 100,000 levels deep with a loop (\u003ccode>for _ in range(99_999): doc = [doc]\u003c/code>). Expected: \u003ccode>json_depth(doc)\u003c/code> returns 100000, while \u003ccode>json.loads(json.dumps(doc))\u003c/code> raises \u003ccode>RecursionError\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>json.dumps\u003c/code> already fails on the way out).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how file systems, the DOM, syntax trees and JSON map onto pre-order, post-order and breadth-first traversals, and how real tools guard against deep trees.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:trees/tree-toolkit-build-walk-serialise-and-property-t":"\u003cp>You will collect this module's templates into one file, write a random tree generator, and check every function against an independent version on thousands of random trees. The result is the toolkit you will use for every tree problem: one line to build a test tree, and a stress test ready to catch your next bug.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.10 or newer, standard library only (if you built the \u003ccode>dsakit\u003c/code> stress harness earlier in the guide, use it for step 4).\u003c/li>\u003cli>One file \u003ccode>treekit.py\u003c/code> and one test file \u003ccode>test_treekit.py\u003c/code> in a scratch folder.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Collect the toolkit.\u003c/b> Copy into \u003ccode>treekit.py\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>TreeNode\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>traverse\u003c/code> (first card), \u003ccode>build\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>to_list\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>level_order\u003c/code> (level-order card), \u003ccode>inorder_iterative\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>preorder_iterative\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>postorder_iterative\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>max_depth_iterative\u003c/code> (iterative card), \u003ccode>serialise\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>deserialise\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>build_from_pre_in\u003c/code> (build card), and \u003ccode>height\u003c/code> (bottom-up card).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Write a random tree generator\u003c/b> that can produce any shape, plus a structural equality check:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\n\n\ndef random_tree(n, rng, values=range(-5, 6)):\n    \"\"\"A random binary tree with n nodes: each new node fills a random empty slot.\"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return None\n    root = TreeNode(rng.choice(values))\n    slots = [(root, \"left\"), (root, \"right\")]\n    for _ in range(n - 1):\n        i = rng.randrange(len(slots))\n        slots[i], slots[-1] = slots[-1], slots[i]      # O(1) removal of a random slot\n        parent, side = slots.pop()\n        child = TreeNode(rng.choice(values))\n        setattr(parent, side, child)\n        slots += [(child, \"left\"), (child, \"right\")]\n    return root\n\n\ndef same(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Structural equality, without recursion.\"\"\"\n    stack = [(a, b)]\n    while stack:\n        x, y = stack.pop()\n        if x is None or y is None:\n            if x is not y:\n                return False\n            continue\n        if x.val != y.val:\n            return False\n        stack += [(x.left, y.left), (x.right, y.right)]\n    return True\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Write the properties.\u003c/b> For each seed from 0 to 1999, make \u003ccode>rng = random.Random(seed)\u003c/code> and a tree with \u003ccode>rng.randint(0, 30)\u003c/code> nodes, then assert:\u003cul>\u003cli>the three iterative traversals equal the three lists from the recursive \u003ccode>traverse\u003c/code>;\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>same(build(to_list(t)), t)\u003c/code> (level-order round trip);\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>same(deserialise(serialise(t)), t)\u003c/code> (marker round trip, including negative values);\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>max_depth_iterative(t) == height(t)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>len(level_order(t)) == height(t)\u003c/code>;\u003c/li>\u003cli>for a tree whose values are made distinct first (relabel the nodes 0, 1, 2, ... in any order), \u003ccode>same(build_from_pre_in(pre, ino), t)\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"4\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Report the smallest failure.\u003c/b> Wrap the loop so that on the first failing seed it prints the seed, the tree as \u003ccode>to_list(t)\u003c/code>, and which property failed. Sort the seeds by tree size if you want the smallest counterexample first.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"5\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Plant a bug and catch it.\u003c/b> In \u003ccode>preorder_iterative\u003c/code>, swap the two \u003ccode>if\u003c/code> blocks so the left child is pushed first. Run the tests and note the first failing tree. Undo the bug.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"6\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Go deep.\u003c/b> Build a chain of 100,000 nodes (each node the left child of the previous one). Check that every iterative function and \u003ccode>same\u003c/code> work on it, then call \u003ccode>traverse\u003c/code> on it inside \u003ccode>try\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>except RecursionError\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"7\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Measure.\u003c/b> Build a random tree with 200,000 nodes and time \u003ccode>traverse\u003c/code> against the three iterative functions with \u003ccode>time.perf_counter\u003c/code>, three runs each, keeping the median.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>All 2,000 random trees pass every property in a second or two.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With the planted bug, almost every seed fails. Sorted by size, the smallest failure has three nodes, a root with two children such as \u003ccode>[-3, -2, 5]\u003c/code>, whose pre-order comes out as \u003ccode>[-3, 5, -2]\u003c/code>. Small random inputs find bugs and explain them at the same time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>On the 100,000-node chain, the iterative functions return 100,000 values and a depth of 100000, \u003ccode>same\u003c/code> returns True, and \u003ccode>traverse\u003c/code> raises \u003ccode>RecursionError\u003c/code> within about the first thousand levels.\u003c/li>\u003cli>On the 200,000-node random tree (height around 45), each traversal takes roughly 0.1 seconds, and the two-push post-order about twice that. The single recursive walk that fills all three lists runs about as fast as one iterative walk; neither style is dramatically faster in CPython, so choose by depth and clarity.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep \u003ccode>treekit.py\u003c/code>: the next module (search trees) and the trie module reuse \u003ccode>TreeNode\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>build\u003c/code> and the stress-test loop.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>","p:104":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Return the maximum number of nodes on a path from the root to a leaf of a binary tree. The empty tree has depth zero. For example, a root with a single child has depth two, not one.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are at most 10,000 nodes, with values from -100 to 100. Values do not affect depth. A tree can be a long chain, so a recursive solution may exceed Python's recursion limit; use an explicit stack instead.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>One number for the whole tree, defined by its longest root-to-leaf chain.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If you already knew the answer for the left and the right subtree, how would you get the root's answer? What is it for an empty tree?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Return answers up the tree\u003c/p>\u003cp>depth(node) = 1 + max(depth(left), depth(right)) with depth(None) = 0: a post-order function that returns its subtree's depth. Counting BFS levels, or a stack of (node, depth) pairs, gives the same answer without recursion on skewed trees.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(h) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each leaf, search from the root for that leaf and count the nodes on its path. Keep the largest count. Repeating the search for many leaves can take O(n²) time. Searching from every leaf separately also obscures that many paths share the same prefix.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Carry the depth already reached with each node. A child is exactly one level deeper than its parent. Each node therefore needs to be visited only once, and the maximum depth seen anywhere is the leaf depth we want. A non-leaf cannot have greater depth than all its descendants.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For root 8 with left child 3 and 3's right child 5:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start at the root\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack = [(8, 1)], answer = 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Process the first two nodes\u003c/p>\u003cpre>8 then 3 give depths 1 and 2; push (5, 3)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Finish at the leaf\u003c/p>\u003cpre>5 has depth 3; answer = 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Push existing children with their parent's depth plus one. The order of left and right visits does not matter because we only take a maximum.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def max_depth(root):\n    if root is None:\n        return 0\n    stack = [(root, 1)]\n    best = 0\n    while stack:\n        node, depth = stack.pop()\n        best = max(best, depth)\n        if node.left is not None:\n            stack.append((node.left, depth + 1))\n        if node.right is not None:\n            stack.append((node.right, depth + 1))\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node is pushed and popped once, so time is O(n). The depth-first stack contains at most one deferred sibling per ancestor, giving O(h) auxiliary space for height h. The input tree is not modified.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count nodes rather than edges and return zero, not one, for an empty tree. A lone root has depth one. Updating only at leaves is also correct, but then make sure a node with just one child is not mistaken for a leaf.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return a deepest root-to-leaf path by retaining parent links.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find minimum depth, treating a missing child differently from a leaf.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:110":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Determine whether every node in a binary tree has left and right subtree heights differing by at most one. Height counts nodes on the longest downward path, and an empty subtree has height zero. An empty tree is balanced.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are at most 5,000 nodes, with values between -10,000 and 10,000. Values are irrelevant. A chain can be much deeper than Python's recursion limit, so an explicit postorder traversal avoids a stack overflow.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A condition must hold at every node, and it compares a measurement of the node's two subtrees.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two booleans from the children cannot decide the parent. What number does the parent also need, and can the same call return it?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Return answers up the tree\u003c/p>\u003cp>Return the subtree's height, or -1 if any node inside is unbalanced. A node returns -1 if either child did or if the two heights differ by more than one, else 1 + max. One pass; calling a separate height function at every node is O(n^2) on a chain.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(h) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At every node, separately compute the heights of its two subtrees and compare them, while checking the same condition recursively for its children. The repeated height calculations revisit descendants. On a chain they sum to O(n²) work, even though a subtree's height never changes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Compute each subtree height once after both children are done. At the same moment check the node's height difference. If any difference exceeds one, return false immediately; otherwise record one plus the larger child height. A missing child contributes zero. The explicit two-phase stack schedules children before their parent.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use the chain 1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3, with each arrow a left child:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Schedule postorder\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Visit 1, 2, 3 before resolving them in reverse order.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Resolve the bottom\u003c/p>\u003cpre>height(3) = 1; height(2) = 2 since its child heights are 1 and 0.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Resolve the root\u003c/p>\u003cpre>child heights at 1 are 2 and 0, so return False.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every stored height belongs to a fully processed subtree, so the local balance test is valid.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def is_balanced(root):\n    heights = {None: 0}\n    stack = [(root, False)]\n    while stack:\n        node, done = stack.pop()\n        if node is None:\n            continue\n        if not done:\n            stack.append((node, True))\n            stack.append((node.right, False))\n            stack.append((node.left, False))\n        else:\n            left = heights[node.left]\n            right = heights[node.right]\n            if abs(left - right) &gt; 1:\n                return False\n            heights[node] = 1 + max(left, right)\n    return True\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Time is O(n), because each node is scheduled and resolved once. The height table costs O(n), and the explicit traversal stack costs O(h), so total auxiliary space is O(n). Here h is tree height. Node objects must be hashable by identity.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Checking only the root is insufficient: an unbalanced descendant can have a balanced-looking root. An empty tree and a single node are balanced. Do not confuse a missing child of height zero with an absent balance check.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the first unbalanced node rather than a Boolean.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Generalize the permitted height difference from one to a parameter.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:543":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Return the diameter of a binary tree: the largest number of edges on a simple path between any two nodes. The path need not include the root. A single node has diameter zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The tree contains 1 to 10,000 nodes, with values from -100 to 100. Shape, not value, determines the answer. Deep chains can exceed Python's recursion limit, so the implementation performs postorder with an explicit stack.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Longest path between any two nodes, and it need not pass through the root.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every path has one highest node where it turns. How long is the longest path that turns at a given node, in terms of its two subtrees?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Global best from local work\u003c/p>\u003cp>A helper returns subtree height in nodes. At each node, left + right is the edge count of the longest path bending there: keep the maximum in an outer variable and return 1 + max(left, right) to the parent, which can only extend one arm.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(h) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every pair of nodes, find the unique path between them and count its edges. There are O(n²) pairs and a naive path search can take O(n) per pair, for O(n³) time. Even recomputing heights at every possible turning point can take O(n²).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every path has a unique highest node. The longest path with that node as its highest point joins its deepest left descendant to its deepest right descendant. If child heights count nodes, their sum is exactly the number of edges in that path. The parent only needs the larger height, not both arms.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For root A with left child B, B's left child D, and right child C:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Resolve the leaves\u003c/p>\u003cpre>height(D) = height(C) = 1; each has candidate diameter 0.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Resolve B\u003c/p>\u003cpre>child heights 1 and 0 give candidate 1 and height(B) = 2.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Resolve A\u003c/p>\u003cpre>child heights 2 and 1 give candidate 3, the path D-B-A-C.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Take the maximum candidate over all nodes, including internal nodes below the root.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def diameter(root):\n    heights = {None: 0}\n    stack = [(root, False)]\n    best = 0\n    while stack:\n        node, done = stack.pop()\n        if node is None:\n            continue\n        if not done:\n            stack.append((node, True))\n            stack.append((node.right, False))\n            stack.append((node.left, False))\n        else:\n            left = heights[node.left]\n            right = heights[node.right]\n            best = max(best, left + right)\n            heights[node] = 1 + max(left, right)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node is visited twice, yielding O(n) time. The stored heights take O(n) auxiliary space; the traversal stack is O(h), where h is height. The implementation uses node identity as a dictionary key.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Do not add one to the candidate: that would count nodes rather than edges. A chain's diameter is its node count minus one. The best path can lie entirely inside a child subtree, so a root-only calculation is wrong.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the endpoints as well as the diameter.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find the weighted diameter when each edge has a nonnegative length.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:94":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Return a binary tree's node values in inorder: all values from the left subtree, then the node, then all values from the right subtree. Return an empty list for an empty tree. The follow-up asks for an iterative traversal.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The tree has 0 to 100 nodes and values between -100 and 100. Values can repeat: their occurrences still belong in the output. Avoid recursion to demonstrate how the call stack can be represented directly.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Values in left-subtree, node, right-subtree order, with a follow-up asking for no recursion.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>While the recursive version is deep inside a left subtree, what is the call stack remembering? Keep those same nodes in a list.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Iterative traversal with an explicit stack\u003c/p>\u003cp>Push the whole left spine, pop a node and record it, then move to its right child and repeat, looping while the stack or the current node is non-empty. The stack holds exactly the ancestors still waiting to be recorded, so it never exceeds the height.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(h) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One direct approach is recursive traversal, building a fresh list at every node by concatenating its left result, its value, and its right result. It gives the correct order, but repeated copying can cost O(n²) on a chain. A shared output list avoids that copying.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The recursion must remember ancestors whose left subtrees are being explored but whose own values have not yet been emitted. Store precisely those nodes on a stack. Descend left until there is no more left child, emit the most recent waiting node, and then explore its right subtree.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let 1 have right child 2, and let 2 have left child 3.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Descend from 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack = [1]; pop 1 and emit it; move to 2.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Descend through 2's left child\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack = [2, 3]; pop 3 and emit it.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Finish\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pop and emit 2; output = [1, 3, 2].\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each time a node is popped its entire left subtree has already been processed, while its right subtree has not yet been visited.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def inorder(root):\n    result = []\n    stack = []\n    node = root\n    while node is not None or stack:\n        while node is not None:\n            stack.append(node)\n            node = node.left\n        node = stack.pop()\n        result.append(node.val)\n        node = node.right\n    return result\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node is pushed and popped once: O(n) time. The explicit stack uses O(h) auxiliary space for tree height h, excluding the O(n) output list.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The outer loop must continue while either the current node or stack is nonempty; a null current node can still leave waiting ancestors. Emit after descending left, not when pushing. Equal values are separate visits.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Produce preorder or postorder by changing when a node is emitted.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Yield values lazily instead of collecting the entire list.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:102":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Return the values of a binary tree grouped by depth, starting with the root's level and proceeding downward. Within each level, list nodes from left to right. Return an empty list for an empty tree. For a root 1 with children 2 and 3, return [[1], [2, 3]].\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The input contains 0 to 2,000 nodes, and values are between -1,000 and 1,000. Values may repeat, so preserve every occurrence. The tree may be skewed or wide. A queue gives a direct representation of the current level and avoids Python's recursion-depth limit.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Output grouped by distance from the root, top row first.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A first-in, first-out line serves nodes in order of depth. How can you tell where one level ends and the next begins?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Level-order traversal with a queue\u003c/p>\u003cp>BFS with a deque. At the start of each round, len(queue) is exactly the number of nodes on the current level, so pop that many, collect their values and push their children for the next round.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(w) space for the widest level w\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>First compute the height. Then, for each level number, start at the root and recursively search for nodes at that depth, collecting them in left-to-right order. Nodes near the top are repeatedly revisited; for a chain the total work is O(n²). The method also requires an extra full traversal to obtain the height.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Breadth-first search visits nodes in increasing depth. Before processing a level, the queue contains exactly its nodes in left-to-right order. Save the queue's current length, remove exactly that many nodes, and append their existing children in left-then-right order. The children form the next level behind the remaining current nodes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For root 1 with left child 2 and right child 3, where 2 has right child 4:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start the first level\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue = [1], width = 1; output [1] and enqueue 2, 3.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Process the second level\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue = [2, 3], width = 2; output [2, 3] and enqueue 4.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Process the last level\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue = [4], width = 1; output [4]; queue is empty.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The saved width is essential: the queue can grow during the loop, but newly added children must wait for the next group. Left-to-right ordering follows from processing parents in that order and adding each left child before its right child.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The queue thus acts as a boundary between work at consecutive depths. Missing children are simply skipped; no placeholder is necessary because the result groups existing nodes rather than reconstructing the shape.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\ndef level_order(root):\n    if root is None:\n        return []\n    queue = deque([root])\n    levels = []\n    while queue:\n        level = []\n        width = len(queue)\n        for _ in range(width):\n            node = queue.popleft()\n            level.append(node.val)\n            if node.left is not None:\n                queue.append(node.left)\n            if node.right is not None:\n                queue.append(node.right)\n        levels.append(level)\n    return levels\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every node enters and leaves the queue once, so time is O(n). Let w be the maximum level width. The queue holds at most parts of two adjacent levels, so auxiliary space is O(w), excluding the O(n) returned groups.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Do not iterate until the queue becomes empty inside a single level: that mixes depths. Do not use a normal list's front deletion, which shifts remaining entries and can make traversal quadratic. The empty root must not be put into the queue as a node.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Reverse alternate level lists for zigzag traversal.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compute the average or maximum value separately for each level.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1448":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count nodes that are at least as large as every value on their root-to-node path. Such a node is called good. The root is always good because the path contains only the root. Equality counts: a node tied with the previous maximum is good.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 1 to 100,000 nodes; values range from -10,000 to 10,000. Negative values and duplicate values are possible. A chain may contain all 100,000 nodes, so use an explicit stack rather than Python recursion.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Whether a node counts depends on the values of every node above it on its root path.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A node does not need the whole path, only one number summarising it. Which number, and how does it change for the children?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Pass path state down the tree\u003c/p>\u003cp>Pass the largest value seen on the path so far down as an argument. A node is good when its value is at least that maximum, and its children receive max(best, val). Return 1 or 0 plus the children's counts.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(h) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each node independently, recover its root-to-node path and scan the values to decide whether that node is maximal on the path. This repeats work on shared prefixes. A naive root search for every node can take O(n²) total time and a path can use O(h) temporary space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The entire ancestor path can be summarized by its maximum value. Pass that maximum down with the node. Compare the node's value with the incoming maximum to decide whether it contributes one, then pass the larger of the two values to both children. Each stack entry has its own path state, so one branch cannot contaminate the other.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Take root 3, left child 1 with child 3, and right child 4.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Visit the root\u003c/p>\u003cpre>incoming maximum = 3; 3 is good, count = 1.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Visit the left path\u003c/p>\u003cpre>1 &lt; 3, so it is not good; its child 3 equals the maximum and is good.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Visit the right child\u003c/p>\u003cpre>4 &gt;= 3, so it is good; final count = 3.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The initial maximum can be the root's own value, which makes the root qualify without relying on a particular numeric lower bound. Inductively, each child's incoming maximum includes exactly its ancestors.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This invariant also explains why the test happens before updating the maximum: it compares the current value against the path above it. Updating first would give the same result with a non-strict comparison, but obscures which values have already been visited.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def good_nodes(root):\n    if root is None:\n        return 0\n    stack = [(root, root.val)]\n    count = 0\n    while stack:\n        node, best = stack.pop()\n        if node.val &gt;= best:\n            count += 1\n        best = max(best, node.val)\n        if node.left is not None:\n            stack.append((node.left, best))\n        if node.right is not None:\n            stack.append((node.right, best))\n    return count\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node is processed once, giving O(n) time. A depth-first stack holds at most one deferred sibling per ancestor, so auxiliary space is O(h), with h the height. The input tree is not modified.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use greater-than-or-equal, not strict greater-than. Starting with zero would incorrectly reject negative roots; using the root value handles negative values naturally. Do not keep one global running maximum across branches, since sibling paths do not include one another.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the good node objects rather than only their count.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count nodes smaller than or equal to every ancestor by tracking a path minimum.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:437":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count nonempty downward paths whose node values sum to a target. A path may begin and end at any nodes, but every step must go from a parent to one of its children. The path need not start at the root or end at a leaf. Different starting or ending nodes define different paths, even when their values match.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 0 to 1,000 nodes, node values range from -1,000,000,000 to 1,000,000,000, and the target ranges from -1,000 to 1,000. Negative values and zero mean that exceeding the target does not justify pruning. Python integers avoid overflow in accumulated sums, and an explicit stack handles a chain near the recursion limit.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count downward paths with a given sum that may start and end at any node, with negative values allowed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A downward path from u to v sums to (root-to-v sum) minus (root-to-parent-of-u sum). Where have you counted subarrays with a given sum before?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Pass path state down the tree\u003c/p>\u003cp>Walk down with the running root-path sum and a Counter of the prefix sums on the current path, seeded with {0: 1}. At each node add count[running - target], then increment count[running], recurse, and decrement it on the way back so sibling branches never pair up.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Start a downward traversal at every node. Accumulate the sum as the traversal goes down, counting each endpoint where it equals the target. There are n possible starts, each potentially reaching O(n) descendants, for O(n²) time on a chain and O(h) traversal space. It is important to continue after finding a match, since later negative or zero values can yield another matching endpoint.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let a prefix sum be the sum from the root through a node, with a virtual zero prefix before the root. A downward path ending at the current node has sum target exactly when an earlier prefix on its current root path equals current sum minus target. A frequency map counts all such earlier prefixes, including duplicates.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The map must contain only prefixes of ancestors, not prefixes from previously visited sibling branches. Increment the current sum before visiting children and remove it after both children are complete. An exit frame implements this backtracking even without recursion. Seed the map with one zero so paths starting at the root are counted.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For root 1 with children -1 and 1, target 1:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Enter root 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sum = 1; frequency[0] = 1, so count = 1; record prefix 1.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Enter left child -1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sum = 0; frequency[-1] = 0, so count stays 1; remove prefix 0 on exit.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Enter right child 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sum = 2; frequency[1] = 1, so count = 2; then remove its prefix.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The second counted path is the right child by itself. Removing the left prefix before visiting the right branch prevents invalid cross-branch paths. Looking up before incrementing also prevents an empty path when the target is zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import defaultdict\n\ndef path_sum_count(root, target):\n    frequency = defaultdict(int)\n    frequency[0] = 1\n    stack = [(root, 0, False)]\n    answer = 0\n    while stack:\n        node, prefix, leaving = stack.pop()\n        if node is None:\n            continue\n        if leaving:\n            frequency[prefix] -= 1\n            if frequency[prefix] == 0:\n                del frequency[prefix]\n            continue\n        current = prefix + node.val\n        answer += frequency.get(current - target, 0)\n        frequency[current] += 1\n        stack.append((node, current, True))\n        stack.append((node.right, current, False))\n        stack.append((node.left, current, False))\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node is entered and exited once, giving O(n) expected time with hash-map operations. The active prefix map and depth-first stack use O(h) auxiliary space, where h is height; in a chain this is O(n). Zero-count entries are deleted so completed branches do not accumulate in the map.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Store frequencies, not just a set, since equal prefixes can occur at different depths and correspond to distinct paths. Remove the current prefix on exit, not immediately after pushing children. Negative values make early stopping invalid. An empty tree returns zero; a zero-valued node with target zero contributes one nonempty path.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count paths with a sum in a range using a data structure over ancestor prefix sums.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count only root-to-leaf target-sum paths by checking leaf status.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:236":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a binary tree and two distinct node references p and q, return their lowest common ancestor: the deepest node having both targets in its subtree. A node is considered a descendant of itself, so if p is an ancestor of q, the answer is p. The tree is not necessarily a search tree.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 2 to 100,000 nodes, all node values are distinct and in the range -1,000,000,000 to 1,000,000,000. Both targets are distinct and are guaranteed to be present. The height may be 100,000, so an iterative approach is safer than recursive Python. Work with node identity rather than relying on values.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The deepest node that has both given nodes below it (or is one of them), with child pointers only and no ordering of values.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Let each call report whether it found p, q or both below it. At which node can a call be sure it is the answer?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Lowest common ancestor and parent maps\u003c/p>\u003cp>Return the node itself if it is None, p or q; otherwise recurse into both sides. If both sides return something, p and q are split across this node, so it is the answer; otherwise return the non-empty side, which carries p, q or the answer upward unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(h) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each candidate node, search its subtree separately for p and q, and choose the deepest candidate containing both. Each subtree search can take O(n); across n candidates this can take O(n²) time. Depth information is also necessary to distinguish a lowest common ancestor from a higher common ancestor.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Traverse from the root once to record each node's parent. Then walk from p upward to the root, placing its ancestors, including p itself, in a set. Walk from q upward until reaching the first member of that set. That first shared node is the deepest common ancestor: q encounters its ancestors in order from deepest to shallowest, and membership means the node also lies on p's ancestor chain.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The parent map converts upward movement, which child-only tree nodes do not provide, into constant-time steps. Stop the initial search as soon as both target nodes are discovered, because their entire ancestor chains are already recorded.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let root A have left child B and right child C; B has children D and E. Query D and E.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Record parents\u003c/p>\u003cpre>parent(B) = A, parent(C) = A, parent(D) = B, parent(E) = B.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Walk from D\u003c/p>\u003cpre>ancestor set = {D, B, A}.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Walk from E\u003c/p>\u003cpre>E is not in the set; its parent B is, so return B.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>If the query instead uses B and D, B is already in its own ancestor set, and D's upward walk reaches B. No special ancestor case is needed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def lowest_common_ancestor(root, p, q):\n    parent = {root: None}\n    stack = [root]\n    while p not in parent or q not in parent:\n        node = stack.pop()\n        for child in (node.left, node.right):\n            if child is not None:\n                parent[child] = node\n                stack.append(child)\n    ancestors = set()\n    while p is not None:\n        ancestors.add(p)\n        p = parent[p]\n    while q not in ancestors:\n        q = parent[q]\n    return q\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Building parent links visits at most n nodes, and the two ancestor walks cost O(h), so time is O(n). The parent map and traversal stack take O(n) auxiliary space in the worst case; the ancestor set uses O(h). Nodes must be hashable by identity. The algorithm relies on both targets being present.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Do not assume the tree is ordered by value. Compare node objects, not just their values, and include each target in its own ancestor chain. If target presence is not guaranteed, detect an exhausted search and define a separate missing-target result rather than popping an empty stack.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Answer many queries after preprocessing ancestors for binary lifting.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Handle missing targets explicitly and report when no valid answer exists.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:105":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reconstruct a binary tree from its preorder and inorder lists. Preorder visits root, left subtree, then right subtree. Inorder visits left subtree, root, then right subtree. Return the root of any tree with those traversals; under the stated uniqueness assumptions its shape is determined.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The lists have the same length, between 1 and 3,000, and contain the same distinct integer values from -3,000 to 3,000. They are valid traversals of a binary tree. Distinctness makes each inorder position unambiguous. A skewed input can exceed Python's recursion limit, motivating iterative reconstruction.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two traversal lists of the same tree with distinct values, and you must rebuild its shape.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Which list tells you the root? Once you know the root, what does the other list tell you about how many nodes are on each side?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Build and serialise trees\u003c/p>\u003cp>The next pre-order value is the root of the subtree being built, and its in-order index splits the in-order range into left and right. Map value to in-order index once, consume pre-order with an iterator, and build left before right over index ranges, never slicing.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Take the first preorder value as the root, linearly search for it in inorder, split both lists around the resulting left-subtree size, and repeat recursively. On a chain, repeated searches and list slices cost O(n²) time and may use O(n²) total live slice space. The splitting idea is correct, but repeatedly copying and scanning is avoidable.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Preorder supplies nodes in the order they are created. A stack tracks constructed nodes whose inorder visit is still pending. If its top is not the next inorder value, the next preorder node is its left child. Otherwise, pop all ancestors matching the next inorder values: the next preorder node becomes the right child of the last popped node.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The inorder index advances only on a pop. This simulates returning from completed left subtrees without recursion or a value-to-index lookup. Distinct values make the top comparison identify exactly the node whose inorder turn has arrived.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For preorder [3, 9, 20, 15, 7] and inorder [9, 3, 15, 20, 7]:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Create 3, then 9\u003c/p>\u003cpre>3 does not match next inorder 9; attach 9 as 3.left.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Create 20\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pop 9 then 3 as they match inorder; attach 20 as 3.right.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Create 15 and 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>attach 15 as 20.left; pop 15 then 20; attach 7 as 20.right.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The stack's remaining nodes are precisely the open ancestors. Once a matching node is popped, its left side and its own inorder position are complete. The last node popped is the ancestor whose right subtree the next preorder value starts.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class TreeNode:\n    def __init__(self, val, left=None, right=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.left = left\n        self.right = right\n\ndef build_tree(preorder, inorder):\n    if not preorder:\n        return None\n    root = TreeNode(preorder[0])\n    stack = [root]\n    i = 0\n    for value in preorder[1:]:\n        child = TreeNode(value)\n        if stack[-1].val != inorder[i]:\n            stack[-1].left = child\n        else:\n            while stack and stack[-1].val == inorder[i]:\n                parent = stack.pop()\n                i += 1\n            parent.right = child\n        stack.append(child)\n    return root\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node is pushed and popped at most once, so time is O(n). The stack and the preorder slice use O(n) auxiliary space in the worst case, excluding the O(n) output tree. The inorder index only moves forward.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Attach to the last popped node, not necessarily the first: several ancestors may finish together. The algorithm assumes valid, distinct traversals and does not validate malformed inputs. A single node, an all-left chain, and an all-right chain should all reconstruct correctly.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Rebuild from inorder and postorder, processing roots in reverse.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Determine whether arbitrary supplied traversal sequences are consistent before building.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:124":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a nonempty binary tree with integer node values, return the largest sum obtainable along any nonempty simple path. A path is a sequence of nodes joined by parent-child edges; no node may appear twice. It may start and end anywhere and does not have to include the root or a leaf. For example, with root -10, children 9 and 20, and 20's children 15 and 7, the answer is 42 from 15 through 20 to 7.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 1 to 30,000 nodes, each valued from -1,000 to 1,000. Negative values are allowed, including an entirely negative tree. The answer must represent at least one node, so zero is not a valid default for an all-negative input. A tree can be a chain of 30,000 nodes, far deeper than Python's default recursive call limit. An explicit postorder traversal avoids depending on that limit.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Maximum sum over paths that can start and end anywhere, with negative values allowed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Separate the path a node can offer its parent (one direction only) from the best path that turns at the node (both directions). What should a negative branch contribute?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Global best from local work\u003c/p>\u003cp>gain(node) = val + max(0, gain(left), gain(right)) is returned upward, while val + max(0, gain(left)) + max(0, gain(right)) updates a global best that starts at negative infinity, so an all-negative tree returns its largest single value.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(h) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate every unordered pair of nodes as potential endpoints, including a node paired with itself. For each pair, find the unique connecting path, add its node values, and retain the largest sum. A fresh path search can take O(n), producing O(n³) time over O(n²) pairs. Even if paths are calculated more efficiently, considering every pair is unnecessary because many pairs share long path segments.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The path must be simple. In particular, adding three child or parent directions at a branching node would produce a branched structure, not a path. This observation is the reason the information returned upward must differ from the best complete path seen so far.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Choose the unique highest node on a path, meaning the node closest to the root among its members. The path can use at most one descending arm in each child subtree. At a node, a child's best downward gain either helps, if positive, or can be omitted entirely, if negative. Therefore the best path turning at the node is its value plus the positive part of each child's gain.\u003c/p>\u003cp>To extend a path from this node to its parent, however, only one child arm can be used: otherwise the parent edge would create a branch. Return the node value plus the larger of zero and the two child gains. Separately update a global best with the two-arm candidate. Processing nodes in postorder ensures both child gains are available before their parent.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every valid path has a highest node and is bounded by the candidate calculated there. Conversely, each candidate corresponds to an actual simple path. Thus the maximum candidate is exactly the desired answer. Initializing that maximum to negative infinity preserves the best single-node answer when every value is negative.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use the tree with root -10, left child 9, right child 20, and children 15 and 7 under 20:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Resolve the leaves\u003c/p>\u003cpre>gains are 9, 15, and 7; best candidate so far is 15.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Resolve node 20\u003c/p>\u003cpre>turning candidate = 20 + 15 + 7 = 42; upward gain = 20 + 15 = 35.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Resolve node -10\u003c/p>\u003cpre>turning candidate = -10 + 9 + 35 = 34; global best remains 42.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The downward gain from 20 cannot include both 15 and 7 when connected to -10, although both are legal in the complete path centered at 20. For a single node -3, the candidate and answer are -3; the empty path with sum zero is never offered as the global answer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def max_path_sum(root):\n    gains = {None: 0}\n    stack = [(root, False)]\n    best = float(\"-inf\")\n    while stack:\n        node, done = stack.pop()\n        if node is None:\n            continue\n        if not done:\n            stack.append((node, True))\n            stack.append((node.right, False))\n            stack.append((node.left, False))\n        else:\n            left = max(0, gains[node.left])\n            right = max(0, gains[node.right])\n            best = max(best, node.val + left + right)\n            gains[node] = node.val + max(left, right)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node is scheduled and resolved once, so time is O(n). The gain table requires O(n) auxiliary space and the traversal stack O(h), where h is height. The total is O(n). The implementation keys the table by node identity and assumes a nonempty tree as specified; it does not mutate the tree.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Do not initialize the global answer to zero, because that silently chooses an illegal empty path for an all-negative tree. Do not return both child gains to a parent: that makes a three-way branch. Do not require the path to include the root, a leaf, or two distinct endpoints. A negative node may still be necessary between two sufficiently valuable arms, so do not simply delete all negative nodes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The code clips each child gain, not the current node's value. This distinction allows a negative current node to connect profitable descendants while ensuring an unprofitable entire child arm is omitted. For a skewed tree, the result may start or end at any interior node.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the actual maximizing path by recording each selected child and the best turning node.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find the maximum root-to-leaf sum, where a missing child cannot be chosen as a completed path.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replace node values with edge weights and define whether a zero-edge path is permitted.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:297":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Design a serializer and deserializer for binary trees. Serialization converts a tree to a string; deserialization reconstructs a tree with exactly the same node values and left/right shape. There is no prescribed wire format, but the two operations must agree. For example, a node with only a left child must not decode as a node with only a right child. An empty tree must also round-trip.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The tree contains 0 to 10,000 nodes, with integer values between -1,000 and 1,000. Values can repeat, so values alone do not identify positions. Both negative and multi-digit values must be encoded unambiguously. A 10,000-node chain can exceed Python's recursion limit, so both directions below use explicit stacks. The judge may supply its own node class; the self-contained code includes a compatible class.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Encode any tree, including its gaps, as one string and decode it back exactly.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>One traversal list loses the shape. What could you write down for the empty children so that a single traversal is enough?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Build and serialise trees\u003c/p>\u003cp>Write pre-order with a marker such as # for every missing child. Decode by reading tokens from an iterator in the same order: a value creates a node whose left and then right subtrees are read next, and a marker returns None. Each subtree is a contiguous block of tokens.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One possible approach is to write down the node values and separately record, for each node, the indices of its left and right children. Decoding allocates all nodes and then reconnects the indexed edges. It uses O(n) time and space and is valid, but needs an index assignment, a node table, and careful handling of missing child indices. Merely writing the values of one traversal without shape information is not a valid serialization: different shapes can have the same preorder.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Another conceptual approach is to try every possible tree shape until one matches a list of values, but the number of shapes grows rapidly and the representation still cannot distinguish multiple trees with the same traversal. Explicit null markers directly encode the missing information.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In preorder, write a node's value and then serialize its left and right subtrees. For every absent child, write a dedicated marker, here \u003ccode>#\u003c/code>. A decoder reads a marker as an empty subtree and a value as a node followed by exactly two encoded subtrees. This grammar uniquely determines where each subtree ends.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The iterative decoder keeps a stack of nodes with unfilled child slots. A frame's flag says whether its left or right slot is next. Attach the next token to that slot; when a value creates a node, push a frame for the new node before processing its children. When a frame receives its right child, remove that frame because both of its slots are filled. A newly created right child still gets its own frame.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The serializer uses a stack in the reverse push order: push right before left so left is emitted first. It writes one token for every real node and every missing child. An n-node binary tree has n + 1 missing child slots, hence 2n + 1 tokens, including the single marker for an empty tree.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Take root 1, no left child, and right child -2:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Serialize the root and its gaps\u003c/p>\u003cpre>preorder tokens = [1, #, -2, #, #].\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Decode the first two tokens\u003c/p>\u003cpre>create root 1; attach None to its left slot; its right slot is next.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Decode the right subtree\u003c/p>\u003cpre>attach -2 as 1.right, then fill both of -2's slots with None.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The first marker cannot be confused with the negative number because tokens are comma-separated and \u003ccode>#\u003c/code> is not an integer. A one-token string \u003ccode>#\u003c/code> represents an empty tree. Repeated values are safe because child slots, rather than values, determine placement. Inductively each consumed token fills exactly the next slot in preorder, so both shape and values are restored.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class TreeNode:\n    def __init__(self, val, left=None, right=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.left = left\n        self.right = right\n\nclass Codec:\n    def serialize(self, root):\n        tokens = []\n        stack = [root]\n        while stack:\n            node = stack.pop()\n            if node is None:\n                tokens.append(\"#\")\n            else:\n                tokens.append(str(node.val))\n                stack.append(node.right)\n                stack.append(node.left)\n        return \",\".join(tokens)\n\n    def deserialize(self, data):\n        tokens = iter(data.split(\",\"))\n        first = next(tokens)\n        if first == \"#\":\n            return None\n        root = TreeNode(int(first))\n        stack = [(root, False)]\n        for token in tokens:\n            node, right_slot = stack.pop()\n            child = None if token == \"#\" else TreeNode(int(token))\n            if right_slot:\n                node.right = child\n            else:\n                node.left = child\n                stack.append((node, True))\n            if child is not None:\n                stack.append((child, False))\n        return root\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Both operations process 2n + 1 tokens, so they take O(n) time, treating bounded-size integer conversion as constant time. Serialization stores the output tokens in O(n) space and uses an O(h) traversal stack. Deserialization splits the string into O(n) token storage and uses an O(h) frame stack, excluding the O(n) reconstructed tree. Here h is the tree height. Space remains linear even for a chain.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Do not omit markers: \u003ccode>[1, 2]\u003c/code> alone cannot distinguish a left child from a right child. Push right before left when encoding with a LIFO stack. Do not lose the parent's pending right slot when descending into its left child. The code assumes its input was produced by this serializer; it is not a validator for truncated, extra, or malformed tokens. If untrusted input is required, validate slot counts, token syntax, and resource limits separately.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The empty tree serializes to \u003ccode>#\u003c/code>; a single node has two following markers. A negative sign belongs to a number token, not to the separator or the null marker. The returned tree is newly allocated, not aliased to the original.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Encode in level order with explicit gaps and trim redundant trailing null markers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Define a versioned format and reject malformed or oversized inputs before allocation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Encode an n-ary tree by recording each node's child count.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:863":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a binary tree, a target node in that tree, and a nonnegative integer k, return the values of all nodes exactly k edges from the target. A path may move up to a parent as well as down to a child. Return the values in any order. When k is zero, the target itself is the only answer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 1 to 500 nodes. Node values are distinct integers from 0 to 500; the target is guaranteed to be present, and k is between 0 and 1,000. Since k can exceed the tree's diameter, an empty answer is possible. Node identity is a convenient way to track visits independently of values.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every node at a fixed distance from a target node, including nodes above it and in other branches.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Distances can go up through parents, which the nodes do not store. What if every node also knew its parent?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Lowest common ancestor and parent maps\u003c/p>\u003cp>Build a parent map with one traversal so each node has up to three neighbours, then run BFS from the target for k rounds with a visited set. The frontier after k rounds is the answer.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each node, find its unique path to the target by searching the tree or comparing root-to-node paths, then count the edges. Repeating a linear search for each candidate takes O(n²) time. This is correct even when the path goes through a common ancestor, but repeats much of the same search.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>View each tree edge as undirected. Child pointers provide downward neighbors; a single traversal constructs the missing parent pointer for every node. Then run breadth-first search from the target. Each BFS round crosses exactly one edge, and the queue after k rounds contains precisely the nodes at distance k.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Track visited node objects so the search does not immediately return to the node it came from. A tree has a unique simple path between any two nodes, and BFS first reaches each node along a shortest path. Stopping when the queue is empty handles distances greater than every possible path.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let A have children B and C, and B have child D. Start at B with k = 2.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Build parent links\u003c/p>\u003cpre>parent(B) = A, parent(C) = A, parent(D) = B.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Expand distance zero\u003c/p>\u003cpre>frontier [B] discovers D and A at distance one.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Expand distance one\u003c/p>\u003cpre>D adds nothing new; A discovers C; frontier at distance two is [C].\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The edge back to B is ignored because B was already visited. A different traversal order may change the output order, which the problem permits.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\ndef distance_k(root, target, k):\n    parent = {root: None}\n    stack = [root]\n    while stack:\n        node = stack.pop()\n        for child in (node.left, node.right):\n            if child is not None:\n                parent[child] = node\n                stack.append(child)\n    queue = deque([target])\n    seen = {target}\n    distance = 0\n    while queue and distance &lt; k:\n        for _ in range(len(queue)):\n            node = queue.popleft()\n            for neighbor in (node.left, node.right, parent[node]):\n                if neighbor is not None and neighbor not in seen:\n                    seen.add(neighbor)\n                    queue.append(neighbor)\n        distance += 1\n    return [node.val for node in queue]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Parent construction and BFS each visit every node at most once, so time is O(n), including when k is much larger than the tree height. The parent map, visited set, and queue use O(n) auxiliary space. Node objects must be hashable by identity.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Do not restrict the search to the target's descendants: answers may be ancestors or lie in sibling subtrees. Mark nodes visited when enqueuing, not after repeatedly rediscovering them. For k zero, return the target value without expanding; for excessive k, return an empty list.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return all nodes within distance k rather than exactly at distance k.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Answer many target-distance queries after preprocessing an appropriate tree index.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>"}]);
